hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17546","From Humayd ibn Ruwayman al-Shami, from al-Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: Abu Bakr and Umar would not execute a man for his slave; they would flog him one hundred lashes, imprison him for a year, and deprive him of his share with the Muslims for a year, when he killed him deliberately. He said: And my father informed me, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, the like of it. He said: And he is ordered to free a slave."
"17547","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, he said: A Muslim is not subjected to retaliation for a slave."
"17548","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Amr ibn Dinar: A slave for a slave? He said: I hold that a freeman is not killed for a slave, but a slave is killed for a slave."
"17549","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The wounds of slaves are valued against their prices, in proportion to the wounds of freemen against their blood-money. Al-Zuhri said: And some of the scholars say: Slaves and slave-girls are a commodity among commodities, so one considers how much that diminished their prices."
"17550","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: The blood-money of an umm walad, even if she bore a child to her master, is the blood-money of his mother until her master dies."
"17551","From Mamar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The wounds of slaves short of life are accidental; but if it is the life, retaliation is taken for him."
"17552","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: Retaliation applies in all of that, and they said: The practice for slaves is like the practice for freemen in retaliation."
"17553","From al-Thawri, concerning two slaves one of whom killed the other, he said: They are not ranked against each other even if one of them was better than the other."
"17554","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a slave who killed a slave deliberately, the slain being better than the killer, he said: He is killed for him."
"17555","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a slave worth a thousand dinars who gouged the eye of a slave worth a thousand dinars, he said: If he gouged his eye deliberately, then retaliation; if by mistake, then blood-money. And if the one who was better had his eye gouged, his price is binding, and nothing but that is upon his family."
"17556","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Salim ibn Abdillah, he said: When a slave is wounded through a freeman, the wound of the freeman is assessed against the price of the slave; if the family of the slave wish, they ransom him with the compensation of the freeman's wound, and if they wish, they surrender him, even if it reaches the life of the freeman."
"17557","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: And the compensation of a slave is in his price, like the compensation of a freeman in his blood-money."
"17558","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning four slaves who killed a slave deliberately, he said: If the master of the slave wishes, he kills them, and if he wishes, he puts them to work."
"17559","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, concerning a slave whose leg is cut off, he said: Half his price."
"17560","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If a man struck another man's boy and cut off his nose, or his ear, or paralyzed his hand, he is handed over to him, and he pays his owner the like of him."
"17561","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A slave killed a slave by mistake, the killer being worse than the slain. He said: If the family of the killer wish, they surrender their slave, or they pay the price of the slain, whichever they wish. And if the killer was better than the slain, likewise for them, whichever they wish."
"17562","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a slave who killed a slave by mistake, he said: If the family of the killer wish, they ransom their slave with the price of the slave who was killed, and if they wish, they surrender him for his offense, even if he was better than him, likewise."
"17563","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A slave kills a slave deliberately, the slain being better than the killer? He said: The family of the slain have nothing but the killer of their slave. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said it too. He said: If they wish, they kill him, and if they wish, they enslave him."
"17564","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: But if the killer was better than the slain, they have nothing but the value of the slain."
"17565","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is the saying of Allah, mighty and exalted: The freeman for the freeman and the slave for the slave? He said: A slave kills a slave deliberately, so he is for him; but if the killer was superior, they have nothing but the value of the slain. From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, the like of the saying of Ata."
"17566","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Why is he not for him, when a freeman is for a freeman? He said: Because the blood-money of two freemen is equal, while two slaves are property, so the value of the one struck. I said: But if a freeman gashes him or gouges his eye? He said: Then his value for what he spoiled, and there is no retaliation from him. So I told him of the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, but he refused all but this saying of his."
"17567","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: That between two slaves there is qisas for deliberate killing in their lives, and for wounds short of that. From Mamar, from Simak, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to that effect."
"17568","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I said to him: A slave strikes a slave, in life or short of it, is there qisas even if they differ in value? He said: No."
"17569","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a slave killed a slave deliberately, and the killer owns property, the property belongs to his master, and his neck is for what he did."
"17570","From Ibn Jurayj, from Salim ibn Abdillah, he said: When a slave acts deliberately, the slave is killed, or if he is wounded through him, it is retaliation."
"17571","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: And a slave is subjected to retaliation from a slave in every deliberate act reaching his life, and for wounds short of that; but if they settle for compensation, then the value of the slain is upon the family of the killer or the wounder."
"17572","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: There is no qisas between two slaves except for life."
"17573","I heard Abu Hanifah narrating, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Whatever wounds of a slave are short of life are ranked like the blood-money of a freeman: for his hand, half his price; for his leg, half his price; for his clarifying wound and his tooth, half a tenth of his price; for his finger, a tenth of his price. But if one of his organs is struck which has no counterpart, his nose cut off, his male organ cut off, or his tongue cut off, then his full price is due, and the one who struck him takes him, he belongs to him."
"17574","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The blood-money of a slave is his price; but if it exceeds that of a freeman, it is reduced to the blood-money of a freeman; a slave is not raised above the blood-money of a freeman. He said: And if the stricken slave was property, it is not counted with his neck in his price."
"17575","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if the masters of the killer wish to ransom their slave with the price of the slain, but the masters of the slain refuse? He said: They may not ransom him; they have nothing but the killer of their slave. If they wish, they kill, and if they wish, they enslave."
"17576","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, he said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyib saying, concerning a slave who is struck: His value on the day he is struck. He said: So we hold that whatever he is struck with is for his master, reckoned from his price. I said: But if both his eyes, or one of them, or his male organ is struck? He said: Then we leave that to his master, and the slave stays with him."
"17577","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim and al-Shabi, they said: A slave is not raised to the blood-money of a freeman. And they said: The slanderer of an umm walad is not flogged."
"17578","From Mamar, from Hammad, he said: He is not taken beyond the blood-money of a freeman."
"17579","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The blood-money of a slave is his price, whatever it reaches, even if it exceeds the blood-money of a freeman."
"17580","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: His price, whatever it reaches; he is but property."
"17581","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim said to me, from Ali and Ibn Masud and Shurayh: His price, even if it falls short of the blood-money of the freeman."
"17582","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a man who has no right hand and cut off another man's left hand, he said: Upon him is the full blood-money, the blood-money of two hands; no retaliation is taken from him."
"17583","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If a man seized a thief to cut his right hand, but his left was cut, it has been carried out upon him; nothing is added to that."
"17584","From al-Thawri, concerning one from whom retaliation is taken in his right hand but who puts forward his left, he said: His right is cut as well."
"17585","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, that Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim informed him, from his father, that he and Ibn al-Musayyib agreed that if a man cut off another man's hand, and a man took retaliation from him but cut the left hand of the cutter: Then the left is sought, and the right is cut. And they said: Retaliation is in its place; and if he cut the left by mistake, its compensation is upon whoever cut it, and the right is cut for the right. Abu Bakr said: And Said ibn Khalid informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of it."
"17586","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote, concerning a man killed leaving a small child, that he wrote: The small one is waited for until he grows up. Sufyan said: Then if he wishes, he takes, and if he wishes, he pardons. Al-Thawri said: And we hold to that; and Ibn Abi Layla and Ibn Shubrumah have waited for him."
"17587","From al-Thawri, from Awf al-Arabi, he said: I met an old man in the days of al-Jamajim, then I left him alone and asked about him, and it was said to me: That is Abu al-Muhallab, the uncle of Abu Qilabah. Then I heard him saying: A man threw a stone at another man's head in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and his hearing, his reason, his tongue, and his male organ went. So Umar decreed four blood-moneys for them, while he was alive."
"17588","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When a man is struck by mistake and both his eyes and his nose are struck, then two blood-moneys; and if his testicles and his male organ are cut, that is two blood-moneys; and likewise in its likes, likewise."
"17589","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a man struck in his limbs, what we vow for him being more than his blood-money? He said: I have heard nothing about it, yet I think he will be given for all he was struck with, even if it is more than his blood-money."
"17590","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, concerning a man who gouged his companion's eye and cut his nose and ear? He said: All that is reckoned for him."
"17591","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that a man who killed a man was brought to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and the kin of the slain came, one of them having pardoned. So Umar said to Ibn Masud, who was beside him: What do you say? Ibn Masud said: I say: He has been secured from killing. He said: Then he struck his shoulder and said: A small vessel filled with knowledge."
"17592","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, that a man who killed a man was brought to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and the kin of the slain wanted to kill him. Then the sister of the slain, who was the wife of the killer, said: I have pardoned my share from my husband. So Umar said: The man is freed from killing."
"17593","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, from Ibrahim, and al-Hajjaj, from Ata, they said: The pardon of everyone entitled to a share is valid."
"17594","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, that a woman whose husband was killed, he having brothers, and one of them pardoned; so Umar ordered blood-money for the rest of them."
"17595","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man killed two men deliberately, then the family of one of them pardoned, and the others did not pardon? He said: He is not killed, but he gives those who did not pardon half the blood-money. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from al-Hasan, the like of the saying of Ata."
"17596","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Talhah, from Ata, about a man who kills deliberately, then one of the sons of the slain pardons and the other refuses? He said: The one who did not pardon is given half the blood-money."
"17597","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When one of the kin pardons, it becomes blood-money, and there falls from the killer the measure of the share of this one who pardoned."
"17598","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz also wrote to that effect. He said: When one of them pardons, then blood-money."
"17599","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: And no ruler prevents the kin of the blood from pardoning if he wishes, or taking compensation when they settle; nor does he prevent him from killing if he refuses all but killing, after killing is established for him in deliberate murder."
"17600","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Pardon belongs to the kin; a woman has no pardon."
"17601","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Women have no pardon in retaliation; but when it is blood-money, she has her share."
"17602","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah: He used not to allow a woman pardon in a hadd or killing, but her pardon is in blood-money and qisas."
"17603","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: It used to be narrated from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: I grant no protection to one who kills after taking blood-money."
"17604","From al-Thawri, concerning one who pardons or takes blood-money, then kills: Allah, blessed and exalted, said: Whoever transgresses after that, for him is a painful punishment. He is the man who kills after taking blood-money."
"17605","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who killed a man, he having two brothers, and one of them pardoned, then the other killed him before he was brought to the imam? He said: It is a mistake; upon him is blood-money, half of it taken from him."
"17606","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah informed me, from al-Thabt, except that he traced it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: He made binding, by oath or otherwise, that no pardon is granted to a man who pardoned blood, then took blood-money, then went in the morning and killed."
"17607","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: And the transgression Allah mentioned is that a man takes compensation, or retaliates, or the ruler judges between the wounder and the wounded, then one of them transgresses after fulfilling his right. Whoever does that has transgressed, and judgment in it belongs to the ruler by what he sees fit of punishment. And even if he were pardoned, none of the seekers of the right may pardon him after his transgression except with the permission of the ruler. And upon that standing is everything of this kind; for it has reached us that this is the matter about which Allah revealed: If you dispute over anything, refer it to Allah and the Messenger, the verse. And whatever wound is above the lesser and below the utmost is assessed by reckoning of blood-money."
"17608","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata saying: If one who is to be killed by mistake grants his blood-money to the one who killed him, he has but a third of it; it is but property he bequeaths."
"17609","From Mamar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: When a man gives his blood in charity and is killed by mistake, a third of that is valid, if he has no other property."
"17610","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When a man gives his blood in charity, and he was killed deliberately, it is valid."
"17611","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: If it was deliberate, it is valid, and it is not from the third."
"17612","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: When a man is struck and gives himself in charity, it is valid. He said: So we said: A third of it? He said: Rather, all of it."
"17613","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, he said: Blood, whatever of it is sold for anything, is valid, even if much."
"17614","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a man who killed deliberately, then they settled for three blood-moneys? He said: Valid; they but bought their companion with it."
"17615","From Mamar, from Said, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, he said: Whatever blood is sold for of anything is valid, even if much."
"17616","From al-Thawri, he said: When he bequeaths that they pardon him, a third is for the aqilah, and he pays two thirds."
"17617","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a man stole, or drank wine, then was killed, it is the killing, nothing is added to that; he is not cut, nor flogged. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, the like of the saying of Ata: It effaced what belongs to people and altered it."
"17618","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When hadd punishments gather upon a man including killing, killing suffices him."
"17619","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: When killing comes, it effaced everything belonging to people, and altered it. Al-Thawri said: And a man informed me, from Ata, the like of it. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr informed me, from more than one, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of the saying of Ata. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard it from Abu Bakr, from Mamar, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of it."
"17620","From Ibn Jurayj, from the companions of Ibn Masud, from Ibn Masud, he said: When killing comes, everything is effaced."
"17621","From some of his companions, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Ibn Masud, he said: When killing comes, it effaced everything."
"17622","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah. He said: The hadd is carried out upon him, then he is killed. From Mamar, from a man, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, the like of it."
"17623","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a man who stole and drank wine, then killed: The hudud are carried out upon him, then he is killed."
"17624","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who killed a man deliberately, then slandered a man, he said: He is flogged, then killed; and if anyone slandered him, he is flogged for him. Al-Zuhri said: But if he stole then killed? He said: He is excused for the theft, and killed. And when hudud gather upon him and he killed, all the hudud are averted from him except slander, for it is carried out upon him."
"17625","From Ibrahim, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When killing becomes due upon a man, and hudud become due upon him, the hudud are not carried out upon him except slander, for he is flogged then killed."
"17626","From al-Thawri, he said: When hudud gather upon a man, then he is killed, whatever belonged to people, take retaliation for it, and whatever belonged to Allah, leave it; killing effaces all that. And Abd al-Razzaq holds to it."
"17627","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi, he said: A man mutilates a man then kills him? He said: He is mutilated as he mutilated him, then killed. Sufyan said: And others said: Killing effaces that, and it is dearer to us."
"17628","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me, that a man struck a man and cut off his nose, and that was brought to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: So Umar gave his heir, and he cut off his nose, then killed him."
"17629","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who kills a man with iron or with something? He said: Retaliation effaces that, with the sword. And Ibn Jurayj said it, from Ata; thus Ibn Jurayj reported it, from Ata."
"17630","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: Ziyad seized a dihqan called Ibn al-Miskin and mutilated him. He said: So Alqamah said: It used to be said: No one has a better killing than the Muslim; we were forbidden the tumults of the market and the tumults of the night, meaning tumults when there is fighting, or crowds in fighting."
"17631","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: Ibn Masud said: The most chaste of people in killing are the people of faith."
"17632","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas ibn Malik, that a Jewish man killed a slave-girl of the Ansar for her jewelry, then threw her into a well and crushed her head with stones. So he was brought to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered that he be stoned until he dies. So he was stoned until he died."
"17633","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Hudud are not carried out in the mosque."
"17634","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard that it is forbidden to confine in the mosque for execution."
"17635","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade retaliation for wounds in the mosque."
"17636","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: A man is not subjected to retaliation from his son in killing."
"17637","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: A man was brought to Umar for something, and he said: Take him out of the mosque and beat him."
"17638","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: I saw al-Shabi flog a Jew a hadd in the mosque."
"17639","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: He was asked about beating in the mosque, and he said: The mosque has sanctity."
"17640","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to him: Do not judge in the mosque, for there comes to you the menstruating woman and the polytheist."
"17641","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who entered another man's house, there being a knife in the house, and he stepped on it and it wounded him, he said: Nothing is upon the owner of the house."
"17642","From Mamar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, that a man was trimming the mustache of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he startled him so he broke wind. He said: We did not intend this, but we shall pay compensation to you for it. So he gave him forty dirhams. He said: And I think he said: And a sheep or a young goat."
"17643","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Uthman decreed for one beaten until he defecates a third of blood-money. Sufyan said: And it is not upon the aqilah."
"17644","From Mamar, and Muhammad ibn Yahya, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, that a man struck a man until he defecated, and he disputed with him to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz. So Umar sent to Ibn al-Musayyib asking him about that: Was there a past practice in this? Ibn al-Musayyib said: Tell him that that occurred in the time of Uthman, and Uthman fined him forty young she-camels."
"17645","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Marwan decreed in that a third of blood-money."
"17646","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd Rabbihi saying: A man called Ibn al-Uqab of Banu Amir lampooned Banu Abs, then he and a man of Banu Abs disputed to - Abd Rabbihi doubted - then Ibn Jurayj said: Ismail ibn Umayyah said: To Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz. Abd Rabbihi said: The Absi said: I did strike him until he defecated. Ibn al-Uqab said: By Allah, he did, but I have no proof, and I was ashamed to mention it; but since he confessed it against himself, take my right. So he asked Ibn al-Musayyib about that, and he said: In it are forty prescribed camels, meaning young she-camels."
"17647","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ayyub informed me, from Ibn Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, that Abd al-Hakam ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Farwah informed him, that Ibn al-Uqab claimed from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz - he said: While I was in the house - against a man who struck him and trampled him until he defecated. Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz saw Sulayman ibn Yasar in the house, called him and asked him, but found no knowledge with him. So he sent a guard to Said ibn al-Musayyib, who returned to Umar with something I do not know what it was. He said: When we went out, we asked what Ibn al-Musayyib had returned to him with? He said: Uthman decreed, concerning a man who struck a man and trampled him until he defecated, forty prescribed camels. Ibn al-Musayyib said: And I saw those camels Uthman decreed with, marked with a ring bearing a line."
"17648","From Sufyan al-Thawri, concerning a man who killed a man deliberately, owing a debt, and the creditors said: We take the blood-money, while the heirs said: We kill? He said: If the heirs wish to kill, they kill; and if the heirs take, the creditors have their debt in the blood-money."
"17649","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, from Jundub ibn Abdillah, he said: I sat with him in the governorship of al-Musab, and he said: These people have plunged into their blood, contended over the world, and vied in building. I swear by Allah, only a little shall come upon you before a controlling camel, a lamb, and a saddle become dearer to one of you than a great estate. Know that I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Let not a handful of the blood of a Muslim man, shed without right, come between one of you and Paradise while he sees its gate. Lo, whoever prays the dawn prayer is under the protection of Allah, so let not Allah demand you for anything of His protection."
"17650","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Bashir ibn Abd al-Harith ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr ibn Makhzum informed me: He was the judge of Quraysh in the Jahiliyyah, and the first who judged in the Jahiliyyah by qasamah, concerning a man who killed another, with a hundred camels; and the compensation of the people of Jahiliyyah was sheep. He said: And the first who ransomed was Abd al-Muttalib; he had vowed, if ten males of his loins were completed for him, he would sacrifice one of them. So they were completed, and he ransomed him with a hundred camels."
"17651","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Qasamah existed in the Jahiliyyah, then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, confirmed it for the Ansari found slain in the well of the Jews. The Ansar said: The Jews killed our companion. And from Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from a man of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, of the Ansar, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the Jews, beginning with them: Will fifty of you swear? They said: No. So he said to the Ansar: Will you swear? They said: Do we swear over the unseen, O Messenger of Allah! So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made it blood-money upon the Jews, because he was found among them."
"17652","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: The first who made swear by qasamah, they claim, was Umar, in blood, fifty oaths."
"17653","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, about qasamah in blood. He said: Qasamah existed in the Jahiliyyah. And from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from a man of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, of the Ansar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, confirmed it upon what it had been in the Jahiliyyah, and judged by it among some people of the Ansar concerning a slain man they claimed against the Jews. He said: And Ibn Shihab informed me about the practice of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in it: That it is upon those claimed against and their kin; fifty men of them swear, when there is no proof to be taken; but if one man of them declines, their qasamah is returned, and the claimants take it, swearing the like of that. If fifty of them swear, they establish right; and if their qasamah falls short, or one of them turns back, blood is not granted them."
"17654","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Fadl informed me, from al-Hasan, that he informed him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, began with the Jews, but they refused to swear. So he returned qasamah to the Ansar, but they refused to swear. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, placed the compensation upon the Jews."
"17655","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar informed me, from their companions, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz began with those claimed against, then charged them the compensation."
"17656","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, and from Yahya ibn Said, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, began with the Ansar. He said: Swear. But they refused to swear. So he said to the Ansar: Will the Jews swear for you? The Ansar said: What do the Jews care about swearing! So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, paid his blood-money from his own means, a hundred camels."
"17657","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Said and others, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that this slain man was at Khaybar, that he was Ibn Sahl of the Ansar, and that he was the brother of Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl. Then he came with Muhayyisah and Huwayyisah, the two sons of Masud, they being cousins of the two sons of Sahl. They came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl spoke before Muhayyisah and Huwayyisah because he was his brother, though he was younger than them. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Quiet! Let the elder speak, meaning the eldest speaks. He said: And Malik said: From Yahya ibn Said, from Bushayr ibn Yasar he informed him, that Abdillah ibn Sahl and Muhayyisah ibn Masud went to Khaybar, separated for their needs, and Abdillah ibn Sahl was killed. Muhayyisah fled, then he and his brother Huwayyisah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abd al-Rahman went to speak for his place as his brother, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Let the elder, let the elder speak! Then Muhayyisah and Huwayyisah spoke, mentioning the affair of Abdillah ibn Sahl. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to them: Will you swear fifty oaths and establish your killer, or your companion? They said: O Messenger of Allah! We did not witness nor attend. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Then the Jews will exonerate you with fifty oaths. They said: O Messenger of Allah! How do we accept the oaths of disbelieving folk?! He said: Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, paid his blood-money from his own means. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, the like of it."
"17658","From Abdillah ibn Saman, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm informed me, from a group of the Ansar, that Abdillah ibn Sahl al-Ansari was killed at Khaybar, he being the first in whom qasamah occurred in Islam. He and Muhayyisah ibn Masud went to Khaybar, separated for their needs, and Abdillah ibn Sahl was killed. Muhayyisah arrived, then he and his brother Huwayyisah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl, the brother of the slain, went to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abd al-Rahman wanted to speak for his place as his brother, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Let the eldest speak! Then Muhayyisah and Huwayyisah spoke, saying: O Messenger of Allah, we found Abdillah ibn Sahl slain in a well of Khaybar, we know not who killed him, and we think it was the Jews. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Will you swear fifty against fifty men that the Jews killed him, so you establish right thereby? They said: O Messenger of Allah! How over a matter absent from us which we did not attend? When they declined, he said: Then the Jews will swear for you and exonerate you, fifty men of them over fifty oaths, that they are innocent of killing your companion. They said: O Messenger of Allah! How do we accept the oaths of the Jews when they are disbelievers? So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, paid his compensation from his own means, a hundred camels. Abu Bakr said: Then Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah al-Ansari informed me: I saw that compensation with which the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, paid for Abdillah ibn Sahl, and a prescribed camel of it kicked me."
"17659","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that qasamah in blood remained upon fifty men; if their qasamah fell short, or one man of them declined, their qasamah was returned. Until Muawiyah performed hajj, and Banu Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza accused Musab ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf al-Zuhri, Muadh ibn Abdillah ibn Mamar al-Taymi, and Uqbah ibn Muawiyah ibn Shub al-Laythi of killing Ismail ibn Habbar. They disputed to Muawiyah when he performed hajj, and Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr established no proof except suspicion. So Muawiyah judged qasamah upon those claimed against and their kin. But Banu Zuhrah, Banu Tamim, and Banu al-Layth refused to swear for them. So Muawiyah said to Banu Asad: Swear. Ibn al-Zubayr said: We swear against all three together and establish right. But Muawiyah refused and said: Swear against one man. Ibn al-Zubayr refused all but swearing against the three, and Muawiyah refused that they swear except against one. So Muawiyah judged qasamah, returning it upon the three claimed against. They swore fifty oaths between the Corner and the Station, and were cleared. That was the first shortening of qasamah. Then in the governorship of Marwan, Ata ibn Yaqub, freedman of Siba, claimed the killing of his brother Rabiah against Ibn Balsanah and his two companions, they being outcasts and sinners. Their kin refused to swear for them, and Marwan did not deem them acceptable to make swear as Muawiyah had made swear. So Marwan made Abdillah ibn Siba and his two sons Muhammad and Ata, sons of Yaqub, swear at the pulpit of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fifty oaths returned upon them. Then he handed Ibn Balsanah and his two companions to them, and they killed them. Abd al-Malik judged like the judgment of Marwan. Then qasamah was returned to the first order. He said: And Mamar used to narrate before that, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr said to Muawiyah: We swear and establish right against them. But he refused them, saying: Swear against one. Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr refused, and Muawiyah refused, so Muawiyah repeated the oaths. He used to narrate this, abridging it. And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it, from Ibn Shihab, the like of it."
"17660","And I heard one saying, and of him the poet says, inciting his people: I shall never answer a caller by night, fearing deception as Ibn Habbar was deceived. Be, O Banu Asad, bearers of honor; never accept less than killing for vengeance. They spent the night dragging him on the ground, face down; evil is the gift to cousin and neighbor."
"17661","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a slain man is found in the courtyard of a people, houses shading over him, then if they swear, they pay blood-money; and if the others swear and they decline, they establish blood; and if both parties decline, then blood-money, because he is among them."
"17662","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If a man is found slain in open land, not near a village, and his killing is not claimed against anyone, there is no blood-money in it. And when a slain man is found in a village, in its farthest or nearest part, he is upon the people of the village."
"17663","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged concerning oaths that the kin swear, then the kin; and if the number of his clan does not reach fifty, the oaths are repeated upon them, however many they are."
"17664","From al-Thawri, from Mujalid ibn Said and Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, that a slain man was found between Wadiah and Shakir. So Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered them to measure between them, and they found him nearer to Wadiah. So Umar made them swear fifty oaths, each man of them: I did not kill, nor know a killer. Then he charged them blood-money. Al-Thawri said: And Mansur informed me, from al-Hakam, from al-Harith ibn al-Azma, that he said: O Commander of the Faithful! Neither did our oaths protect our wealth, nor did our wealth protect our oaths. So Umar said: Such is the right."
"17665","From Ibn Jurayj, from Mansur, from al-Shabi, the like of this, except that he said: He made them enter al-Hatim, then brought them out man by man, and made them swear."
"17666","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Shabi, concerning a slain man found between two villages, he said: The nearer of them to him is taken."
"17667","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Abu Jafar, he said: Detention by the imam after carrying out the hadd is injustice. He said: And Ali said: Any slain man found in a desert land, his blood-money is from the treasury, so that no blood is voided in Islam; and any slain man found between two villages is upon the nearer of them, meaning the nearer."
"17668","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: I witnessed him making a group swear fifty oaths: I did not kill, nor know a killer. He said: And Shurayh says: I do not burden them, while I know. From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, the like of it."
"17669","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ibn Shihab: Is qasamah in blood upon knowledge or upon proof? He said: Rather upon proof."
"17670","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If the qasamah of one man of them falls short, it is returned. He said: Thus was qasamah. He says: It was returned; the oaths were not repeated upon them."
"17671","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that Marwan ibn al-Hakam judged for Banu Junda by qasamah, but both parties declined, so he judged half the blood-money. Mamar said: Blood-money is due only when he perishes on the spot in quasi-deliberate killing. But if he lived after the beating, he remains liable for it until he dies. Then qasamah occurs then: the claimants swear he died of his beating him; if they swear, they establish blood-money; and if they decline, fifty of the others swear he did not die of his beating him. Then it is the blood-money of that wound; and if those claimed against decline, they pay half the blood-money."
"17672","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: A man struck a man with a stick, and he lived a day, saying: So-and-so struck me. Then he died. His people came to Abd al-Malik asking him for retaliation. So he ordered them to swear upon him: Six men of them swore fifty oaths, repeating the oaths upon them. Then he handed him to them in retaliation for their companion."
"17673","From Mamar, he said: I said to Ubaydullah ibn Umar: Do you know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave retaliation by qasamah? He said: No. I said: Then Abu Bakr? He said: No. I said: Then Umar? He said: No. I said: Then how do you venture upon it? So he fell silent. He said: Then I said that to Malik, and he said: We do not place the command of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, upon deceit; had he been tried with it, he would have given retaliation by it."
"17674","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yunus ibn Yusuf informed me, he said: I said to Ibn al-Musayyib: Wonder at qasamah! A man comes asking about the killer and the slain, knowing neither killer nor slain, then swears. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by qasamah for the slain of Khaybar; had he known people would venture upon it, he would not have judged by it."
"17675","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: A freedman of Abu Qilabah related to me, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz visited Abu Qilabah while he was ill, and said: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qilabah! Do not let the hypocrites gloat over us. He said: Then they talked until they mentioned qasamah. Abu Qilabah said: O Commander of the Faithful! These are the nobles of Syria with you, and their notables. Tell me, if they testified that so-and-so stole in such-and-such land, would you cut him? He said: No. He said: If they testified he drank wine in such-and-such land, while they are with you here, would you flog him for their saying? He said: No. He said: Then why, when they testify he killed him in such-and-such land while they are with you, do you retaliate? He said: So Umar wrote concerning qasamah: If they establish two just witnesses that so-and-so killed him, then retaliate for him; and the testimony of one of the fifty who swore is not accepted."
"17676","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz called me, saying: I wish to leave qasamah; a man comes from such-and-such land, and another from such-and-such land, and they swear. He said: So I said to him: That is not for you; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by it, and the caliphs after him. If you leave it, a man is likely to be killed at your door and his blood voided; for people have life in qasamah."
"17677","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man whose killing two brothers were accused of, and their father feared they would be killed, so their father said: I killed your companion, and each of the two brothers said: I killed him, each clearing the other, he said: We hold that to the kin of the slain, to swear qasamah against one of them."
"17678","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Sulayman ibn Hisham wrote to him, asking him about a man found slain in the house of a people. They said: He came upon us to steal from us. His kin said: They lie; rather, they invited him to their house, then killed him. Al-Zuhri said: So he wrote to him: Fifty of the kin of the slain swear that they lie: he did not come to steal from them, they invited him but as invitation, then killed him. If they swear, they are given retaliation; and if they decline, fifty of those swear by Allah he came upon us to steal from us. Then upon them is blood-money. Al-Zuhri said: And Uthman judged thereby concerning Ibn Bamirah al-Maami; his people refused to swear, so he charged them blood-money."
"17679","From al-Thawri, he said: When a slain man is found among a people with a trace on him, his compensation is upon them; and when there is no trace on him, nothing is upon the aqilah, unless proof stands against someone. Sufyan said: And this is what is agreed upon with us."
"17680","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: If a man is killed beside a people, or in open land, and a trace is found with him, and there was suspicion or stain with him: if the qasamah of those claimed against does not suffice, or a man of them declines, or the qasamah of the claimants does not suffice, or a man of them declines, then compensation is upon them, because he was killed beside them, and because of suspicion. But if he was not killed beside a people, no trace found with him, no suspicion with him, and the qasamah of those claimed against does not suffice, or a man of them declines, or the qasamah of the claimants does not suffice, or a man of them declines, then blood is voided and lost. He said: Such was the first order; but what people follow today is that oaths are repeated."
"17681","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Amr, from al-Fudayl, from Ibrahim, he said: When a slain man is found among a people, then two witnesses testify against one that he killed him; otherwise they swear fifty oaths and pay blood-money. Sufyan said: This is what we take in qasamah."
"17682","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: When they do not complete fifty, the oaths are repeated upon them."
"17683","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Qasamah obliges compensation, but does not shed blood."
"17684","From Ismail ibn Abdillah Abu al-Walid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, that two men came to Umar at Mina, saying: A cousin of ours to whom we are close was killed. So Umar said: Two just witnesses against one that he killed him, and we hand him to you; otherwise, those around you swear they did not kill. If they decline, you swear fifty oaths, then yours is blood-money. Qasamah obliges compensation, but does not shed blood."
"17685","From Mamar, from Amr and others, from al-Hasan, he said: They establish blood-money by qasamah, but do not establish blood by it."
"17686","From Ibrahim, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no qasamah except with proof standing, meaning he says: None is killed by qasamah, nor is the blood of a Muslim voided."
"17687","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged, as it reached us, concerning a slain man found among dwellings, that oaths are upon those claimed against. If they decline, the claimants swear and establish right. But if both parties decline together, blood-money is halved: half upon those claimed against, and half voided by the claimants, since they disliked establishing right by their oaths."
"17688","From al-Thawri, concerning a man found slain in his house, he said: His aqilah guarantees his blood-money."
"17689","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that a man was killed, and his kin claimed his killing against two men who were with him. They disputed to Shurayh, saying: These two killed our companion. Shurayh said: Two just witnesses that they killed your companion. They found none to testify for them, so Shurayh freed the two men. They came to Ali and told him the story. Ali said: May your mother bereave you, O Shurayh! Had the man two just witnesses, he would not have been killed. Then he secluded the two, kept treating them gently and questioning them until they confessed. So he killed them. Ali said: Sad brought it, with Sad wrapped; easiest is swift striving; camels are not brought thus, O Sad!"
"17690","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, that a slain man was found among a people, and his kin claimed against other people. They came to Shurayh: So he cleared the quarter in which he was found slain, and asked his kin for proof against the others they claimed against."
"17691","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who rented his house to a tenant, then a murdered man was found in the house. Ibn Abi Layla said: it is upon the tenant, and he derived it from the people of Khaybar, for he said: they were workers working in a place, and a murdered man was found among them in a house, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the guardians of the blood: Will you swear fifty oaths? They said: How can we swear when we did not see? He said: Then the Jews will swear for you. They said: How can the Jews swear when they are polytheists? So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, paid his blood-money from the charity camels. Sufyan said: And we say: it is upon the owners of the property, meaning the owners of the house."
"17692","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, he said: Shurayh was brought a case about a man found dead on a bench at the door of some people, with no mark on him, so he made the people of the house swear."
"17693","From al-Thawri, from Said al-Yashkuri, from al-Shabi, he said: If the body of the murdered man is found in a house or a place, funeral prayer is offered over him and blood-money is paid; and if a head or a leg is found, no funeral prayer is offered over it and no blood-money is paid."
"17694","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man from Banu Sad ibn Layth made a man from Juhayna mount a horse roughly, and it cut off one of the toes of his foot, and it bled until he died. Umar said to the Juhanis: Will fifty of you swear that he struck him and that he died from it? They refused to swear, so he asked fifty of the others to swear, and they refused to swear, so Umar ibn al-Khattab made it half the blood-money. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, similar to it; he said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz was inclined to this, to the point that he would judge by it even in a matter which he saw was far from it. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik judged likewise in the case of Ibn Nuh and Tamim ibn Mihran, and Hisham in the case of Ibn Sad ibn Said al-Hudhali, when he died from that; and the two had been wrestling."
"17695","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Muslim informed me that a slave-girl bit the finger of a freedman of Banu Abi Zayd and he died, and the girl confessed that she bit him, so Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled that Banu Abi Zayd should swear fifty oaths, repeated upon them, that he died from her bite, then the slave-girl would belong to them, otherwise they have no right; and they refused to swear."
"17696","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Tamim ibn Salama, he said: A man carried another man, then threw him to the ground, and kept striking him with his elbow and beating him until he died, so the case was brought to Shurayh concerning him, and he said: Do you bear witness that he killed him?"
"17697","From al-Thawri, from Hammad and others, he said: If he struck him and he remained ill until he died, he is killed for him."
"17698","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man asked Ata about an insane man who pushed his slave-boy and injured him in something or killed him; he said: His blood is not void. Ata said: A stray stone came during the rule of Marwan and struck Ibn Nistas, the uncle of Amir ibn Abd al-Rahman, and its thrower was not known, and it killed him, so Marwan imposed his blood-money upon the people."
"17699","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: The mother of Umayr ibn Said was married to al-Jullas ibn Suwayd, and al-Jullas said during the expedition of Tabuk: If what Muhammad says is true, then we are worse than donkeys. Umayr heard it and said: By Allah, I fear that if I do not report it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the Quran will come down concerning it and I will be mixed up in his sin, though he is a good father to me. So he informed the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who summoned al-Jullas, and he acknowledged it while they were departing, and the two disputed. Then revelation came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and they fell silent and no one moved, and that is what they used to do; they would not move when revelation descended. Then it was lifted from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: They swear by Allah that they said nothing, yet they certainly spoke the word of disbelief, up to: then if they repent. So al-Jullas said: Let my Lord grant me repentance, for I repent to Allah, and I bear witness that he told the truth. And they resented nothing except that Allah and His Messenger enriched them. Urwa said: A freedman of al-Jullas was killed among Banu Amr ibn Awf, and Banu Amr refused to pay his blood-money, so when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived, he imposed his blood-money upon Banu Amr ibn Awf. Urwa said: Umayr remained ever after in high standing until he died, meaning his wealth increased and he rose above the people, that is by wealth, and that is elevation. Ibn Jurayj said: And I was informed from Ibn Sirin, he said: Umayr never heard anything displeasing from al-Jullas after that."
"17700","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When the Quran came down, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took hold of the ear of Umayr and said: Your ear has fulfilled its duty, O Umayr, and your Lord has confirmed you."
"17701","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled that whenever people of a brawl disperse from a killing or a wound, and that wound of his leads him to death, and the wounded man accuses some of those who struck him to the exclusion of others, and upright people of the brawl who are not known for wrongdoing nor suspected of enmity between him and the accused bear witness to that, then the family of the slain repel by oaths, because of the suspicion they have against the passersby, so they swear fifty oaths by Allah, besides whom there is no god, that so-and-so killed our companion and he died only from his blow."
"17702","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: Some Hudhali people disowned a thief among them who used to rob the pilgrims; they said: We have disowned him, so whoever finds him stealing, his blood is void. A party from Yemen found him stealing from them and killed him, so his people came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and they swore by Allah: We did not disown him, and the people lied against us. So Umar made them swear fifty oaths, then Umar took the hand of a man from the party and said: Attach this man to one of you until you bring the blood-money of your companion. They did so and set out, until when they neared their land, heavy rain befell them, and they sheltered by a long mountain, evening having fallen. When all of them had alighted, the mountain collapsed upon them, and none of them survived, nor any of their mounts, except the detained man and his companion; and he used to narrate what befell his people."
"17703","From Mamar, from Abu al-Zinad, that Umar ibn al-Khattab made a woman swear fifty oaths, then made it the blood-money."
"17704","From Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from Abu al-Zinad, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab made a woman swear fifty oaths concerning a freedman of hers who had been struck down."
"17705","From al-Thawri, he said: There is no qasama upon women and children; he said: And we adopt this."
"17706","From al-Thawri, he said: There is no qasama upon slaves; and we adopt this."
"17707","From Mamar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning a slave whom his elder, a butcher, struck with a sandal or other than it, and he remained ill for days, then died; so he wrote: Make his guardians swear that he died from the blow of his elder, I do not know but that he said fifty oaths, then make him pay his price; and if they refuse, the guardians of the elder who struck shall swear, and if they refuse, make them pay half the price of the slave."
"17708","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: There is no qasama for slaves; they are merely prices like a claimed debt. He said: And I say: Hisham ruled concerning the slave of Ayyub, the freedman of Nafi, with fifty oaths upon Ayyub, so he swore and took his price."
"17709","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said concerning slaves and boys when one of them is injured and there is no proof of that except themselves, and they testify that so-and-so struck him; he said: I do not accept their testimony, but I shall impose his blood-money upon all of them together. It used to be said: If a herdsman among herdsmen is injured, his blood-money is upon them."
"17710","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Whoever is killed in a crowd, his blood-money is upon the people, upon whoever attended that, whether on Friday or otherwise."
"17711","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled that whoever is killed on the day of Fitr or the day of Adha, his blood-money is upon the people collectively, because it is not known who killed him."
"17712","From al-Thawri, from Wahb ibn Uqba al-Ijli, from Yazid ibn Madhkur al-Hamdani, that a man was killed on Friday in the mosque in the crowd, so Ali placed his blood-money from the public treasury."
"17713","From al-Thawri, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that a man was killed in the Kaba, so Umar asked Ali, and he said: From the public treasury."
"17714","From Ibrahim, from Amr, from al-Hasan, that a woman passed by some people and asked them for water, and they did not give her drink, so she died of thirst, and Umar imposed her blood-money upon them. Sufyan said, concerning a man who accepted the testimony of a slave and a freeman against a man and punished him: Upon them, from the public treasury."
"17715","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that Ikrima was asked about a man who swore among fifty men in a qasama over blood, then a man came and swore without knowledge, then came seeking repentance, so Ikrima ruled that he should repent to Allah, pay his share of the blood-money to the family of the slain, and free a slave."
"17716","From Mamar, from Matar, from Ikrima, concerning four who testified against a man for adultery and he was stoned, then one of them retracted; he said: Upon him is a fourth of the blood-money, and he must free a slave."
"17717","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yunus ibn Yusuf informed me that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: Two men fought each other, and one of them said to his companion that he went to strike him, and one of the two bones of his arm was crushed. Ibn al-Musayyib said: Uthman said: When two fighters fight, whatever wounds occur between them are subject to retaliation. Sufyan said concerning two men wrestling, where one wounds his companion; he said: Each of them guarantees his companion."
"17718","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab was asked about one who imposed half the blood-money upon the two wrestlers; Ibn Shihab said: We hold the full blood-money upon the survivor of the two, and that is the sunna as we have known it."
"17719","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning people who fought one another, being neighbours, and a murdered man was found among them; he said: If proof is established against a man that he killed him, retaliation is taken from him; and if no proof is established, the sunna has passed that blood-money is paid for whoever is killed in blind fighting or wounded, when it is not known who killed or wounded him."
"17720","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Shurayh, that a man fell from atop a house onto another man, and the upper one died; Shurayh said: I do not hold the ground liable, so he did not hold the lower one liable for the upper one, though he would hold the upper one liable for the lower one."
"17721","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from a man, from Ali, that he held each of the two liable for his companion."
"17722","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubruma, he said: Whichever of the two dies, his blood-money is upon the other, so he held each of them liable for his companion. He said: And if a man clings to another man, whichever of them dies, his blood-money is upon the survivor."
"17723","From Ibn Shubruma, concerning a man who said to a man: Lower a rope so I may climb it, and he lowered a rope and it broke while he was pulling it; he said: The blood-money is upon him."
"17724","From Ashath, from al-Hakam, from Ali, that two men collided, one striking the other, and he held each of them liable for his companion, meaning the blood-money."
"17725","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, he said: I bear witness concerning Ali that he ruled about people who fought one another and killed one another: he imposed the blood-money of those killed upon those wounded, and deducted from the blood-money according to their wounds."
"17726","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning people who drank and became intoxicated and killed one another; he said: We hold that intoxication does not nullify anything of retaliation; some of them are killed for others, and retaliation is taken among them."
"17727","From Mamar, he said: Hisham ibn Hubayra ruled concerning people who were in water and pushed one another, and one of them died among them in the water, and two testified against three, and three testified against two; so he imposed his blood-money upon all of them."
"17728","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled in doubtful beating by the testimony of a slave and women and the like, that the claimant is made to swear, then he takes retaliation. And Ibn al-Musayyib used to say: No, but he swears, then the blood-money. And I say: The saying of Ibn al-Musayyib is closer to the ruling of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning blood: The accused are made to swear, then they bear the blood-money and escape the blood."
"17729","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Khalid al-Dimashqi informed me that Abd al-Malik ruled concerning the fetus when it is miscarried as a clot: twenty dinars; if it is a lump of flesh, forty; if it is bones, sixty; if the bone has been clothed with flesh, eighty; and if its creation is complete and its hair has grown, one hundred dinars. He said: And it reached me that Ali ruled likewise."
"17730","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: When is the vow of the fetus due? He said: So long as it is not a lump of flesh; I think I said to him: If it is formed but not complete, is its vow due? He said: Yes."
"17731","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: If it is a lump of flesh, two-thirds of a ghurra; and if it is a clot, one-third."
"17732","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If it is a distinct miscarriage, there is a ghurra in it, if it did not cry at birth; but if it cried, its full blood-money is due. If male, one thousand dinars, and if female, five hundred dinars. He said: And Qatada said it also."
"17733","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they both said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled concerning the fetus a ghurra, a slave or a slave-girl."
"17734","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, he said: Two women of Hudhayl fought, and one of them threw a stone at the other, striking her belly and killing her, and she miscarried a fetus. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled her blood-money upon the clan of the killer, and for her fetus a ghurra, a slave or a slave-girl. Then a man said: How can blood-money be paid for one who neither ate nor drank, nor spoke, nor cried? The like of that is void. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said, as Abu Hurayra claimed: This is from the brothers of the soothsayers."
"17735","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Umar consulted about a woman who struck another with a staff and he wanted to apply retaliation to her, then asked whether there was a ruling from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about that. It was said to him: There were two women under Hamal ibn Malik ibn al-Nabigha, and one of them struck the other and killed her and her fetus. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled the blood-money for the woman and a ghurra for the fetus, a slave or a slave-girl or a horse. He said: And he magnified Allah. He said: And Umar adopted that, and said: Had I not heard this, I would have spoken otherwise concerning it. Then the man said: O Messenger of Allah, how can I pay for one who neither ate nor drank, nor spoke, nor cried? And the like of this is void."
"17736","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The ghurra is a slave, or a slave-girl, or a horse. I said: Is this in the hadith of Umar? He said: Yes."
"17737","Ubada said, from al-Hajjaj, from Makhul, from Zayd, he said: If the fetus falls alive, its full blood-money is due, whether it cried or not. And Mamar said, from al-Zuhri: Until it cries; even if it sneezed, it is with me like crying."
"17738","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The ruling of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning that was mentioned to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he sent for the husband of the two women, and he informed him that one of his two wives had struck the other with the tent-pole of the house, killing her and what was in her belly. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled her blood-money and a ghurra for her fetus. So Umar magnified Allah and said: We were almost about to judge in the like of this by our own opinion."
"17739","From Ibn Uyayna, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar stood upon the pulpit and said: I adjure by Allah any man who heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, rule concerning the fetus. So Hamal ibn Malik ibn al-Nabigha al-Hudhali stood and said: O Commander of the Faithful, I was between two slave-girls, meaning two co-wives, and one of them wounded or struck the other with the mistah, the pole of her shelter, killing her and what was in her belly. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled a ghurra, a slave or a slave-girl. Umar said: Allah is Most Great; had we not heard the like of this, we would have judged otherwise."
"17740","Ibn Uyayna said: And Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled therein a ghurra, a slave or a slave-girl or a horse."
"17741","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The ghurra is a slave, or a slave-girl, or one hundred sheep. And Ayyub said, from Abu Malih ibn Usama: One hundred and ten."
"17742","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me that two women of Hudhayl were married to a man of Hudhayl, one of them pregnant, and her co-wife struck her with a stick and she miscarried. Her husband came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and told him the news. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A ghurra, a slave or a slave-girl, for her miscarriage. The cousin of the striker, called Hamal ibn Malik ibn al-Nabigha, said: He neither drank nor ate, nor cried; the like of this is void. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Is it rhymed speech? Or he said: Rhymed speech for the rest of the day."
"17743","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled concerning the woman who struck her companion, killing her and what was in her belly, her blood-money upon the clan, and for her fetus a ghurra, a slave or a slave-girl."
"17744","From Mamar, he said: Said ibn Abi Aruba informed me, he said: I heard Qatada say: Had he come out complete, I would not grant him inheritance until he cries."
"17745","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled concerning the fetus a ghurra, a slave or a female slave. The Hudhali against whom judgment was passed said: How can I pay, O Messenger of Allah, for one who neither drank nor ate, nor spoke, nor cried? The like of that is void. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This is but from the brothers of the soothsayers."
"17746","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, placed the blood-money of the murdered woman upon the clan."
"17747","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Ubayd ibn Nudayla al-Khuzai, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba, he said: She struck a co-wife of hers with the pole of a tent and killed her. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled her blood-money upon the tribe of the killer, and for what was in her belly a ghurra. The Bedouin said: O Messenger of Allah, do you make me pay for one who neither tasted nor drank, nor shouted, then cried? The like of that is void. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Is it rhymed speech like the rhymed speech of the Bedouins?"
"17748","And I heard another mention, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The ghurra is upon the clan."
"17749","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa informed me, from Urwa, that he narrated from al-Mughira ibn Shuba a hadith, from Umar, that he consulted them about the miscarriage of a woman. Al-Mughira said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled therein a ghurra. Umar said to him: If you are truthful, bring someone who knows that. So Muhammad ibn Maslama testified that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, rule therein a ghurra."
"17750","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled concerning a fetus killed in the belly of the woman a ghurra: for the male a boy-slave, and for the female a slave-girl."
"17751","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled concerning a woman killed while pregnant: her blood-money, and a slave or a slave-girl for her fetus."
"17752","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrima, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that the name of the Hudhali whose one wife killed the other: So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled a ghurra for the fetus and blood-money for the woman. His name is Hamal ibn Malik ibn al-Nabigha, of Banu Kathir ibn Khunasa ibn Ghafila ibn Kab ibn Tabikha ibn Lihyan ibn Hudhayl; and the name of the killer woman is Umm Afif, daughter of Masruh, of Banu Sad ibn Hudhayl, and her brother is al-Ala ibn Masruh; and the murdered woman is Mulayka, daughter of Uwaymir, of Banu Lihyan ibn Hudhayl, and her brother is Amr ibn Uwaymir. Al-Ala ibn Masruh said: He neither ate nor drank, nor spoke, nor cried, so the like of this is void. Amr ibn Uwaymir said: Indeed it was a male. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled for the fetus a ghurra: a male or a female slave, or a horse, or one hundred sheep, or ten camels. All this is from Ikrima, the freedman of Ibn Abbas."
"17753","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: The value of the ghurra is fifty dinars. From Mamar, from Ibn Shubruma, the like of it."
"17754","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The fetus neither inherits, nor is its blood-money complete until it cries; but if it sneezes, it is with me like crying."
"17755","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is upon one who kills one who did not cry? He said: I hold he should free a slave or fast."
"17756","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who struck his wife and she miscarried; he said: He pays a ghurra, and upon him is freeing a slave, and he does not inherit from that ghurra; it belongs to the heir of the child other than him."
"17757","From Umar ibn Dharr, he said: I heard Mujahid say: A woman wiped the belly of a pregnant woman and she miscarried a fetus, so that was raised to Umar, and he ordered her, meaning the one who wiped, to expiate by freeing a slave."
"17758","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, concerning a woman who drinks medicine or inserts something so her child falls; he said: A ghurra expiates for her."
"17759","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The fetus of the slave-girl is valued by the price of its mother, like the fetus of the free woman in the blood-money of its mother."
"17760","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning the fetus of the slave-girl: If alive, its price; and if dead, half a tenth of the price of its mother."
"17761","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, concerning the fetus of the slave-girl: half a tenth of the price of its mother. Sufyan said: And our saying is: If it comes out alive, its price is due; and if it comes out dead, half a tenth of the price of its mother, what it would have been alive."
"17762","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who freed the fetus of his slave-girl, then the slave-girl was killed; he said: Blood-money is paid for the slave-girl, and for her fetus as a slave, for the completion of its freeing was that it be born and cry loudly."
"17763","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Concerning the fetus of the slave-girl: ten dinars. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ismail ibn Umayya, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of it."
"17764","From Mamar, from one of the Kufans, concerning the fetus of the slave-girl: its value in proportion, were it alive, to the blood-money of the fetus of the free woman."
"17765","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One of them said: According to the value of its mother, as with the fetus of the free woman according to her blood-money alive. And I say: That was not valued by the mother, nor valued by the father. Ziyad ibn Shaykh said: According to the fetus of the free woman from its blood-money; had it been alive then killed, there would be twelve thousand in it, but it was killed in its mother's belly, so in it is a ghurra; this is from its blood-money. He said: And the fetus of the slave-girl, had it come out and been killed, its price would be fifty dinars and the like, but it was killed as a fetus, so in it is of that proportion. Had it been said from the value of its mother, its value would be more than its price had it come out and been killed."
"17766","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said to me: The stallion is void of liability, the mine is void of liability, and the well is void of liability."
"17767","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dumb animal is void of liability, the well is void of liability, the mine, its wound is void of liability, and in buried treasure is the fifth."
"17768","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yaqub ibn Utba and Salih and Ismail ibn Muhammad, they claimed that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled that the dumb animal is void of liability, the well is void of liability, the mine is void of liability, and in buried treasure is the fifth. He said: And the people of ignorance used to hold the tribe liable for what their beasts, wells and mines struck, so when that was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he gave that ruling which he gave."
"17769","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, wherein: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said concerning two men, one struck down by a mine and the other killed by a beast: What the mine kills is void of liability, and what the dumb beast kills is void of liability. And jubar in the speech of the people of Tihama is void."
"17770","From al-Thawri, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The mine is void of liability, the pasturing animal is void of liability, in buried treasure is the fifth, and the leg is void of liability, meaning the leg of the beast is void."
"17771","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If a man is attacked by a stallion and the man kills it? He said: The man pays for it. He said: I said to al-Zuhri: Why? He said: Because the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The dumb animal is void of liability for its wound. Al-Zuhri said: And whoever harms the dumb animal for something, he pays."
"17772","From Mamar, from a companion of his, from Abu al-Muhazzim, from Abu Hurayra, he said: He pays if he harms the dumb animal."
"17773","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, he said: A stallion attacked a man, so he struck it with the sword and killed it, and that was mentioned to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and he said: I make him pay for a beast that does not reason. And Ali said the like of that."
"17774","From Mamar, from Hammam, from Abu Hurayra, he said: Whoever harms the dumb animal, he pays."
"17775","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from elders of theirs, that a boy entered the house of Zayd ibn Suhan, and a she-camel of Zayd struck him and killed him, so the guardians of the boy set out and hamstrung it. They disputed to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he nullified the blood of the boy and made the father pay the price of the she-camel."
"17776","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that a camel bolted and struck a man and killed him, so the guardians of the slain hamstrung it. They disputed to Shurayh, and he nullified the blood of the slain and made them pay the price of the camel."
"17777","From Mamar, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: A noble she-camel struck a boy and killed him, so the family of the boy came and killed the she-camel. Shurayh made them pay the price of the she-camel and nullified the blood of the boy."
"17778","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I cannot hold back the stallion except by killing it, how can I pay for it? He said: They have said that, and I think a sunna has passed in it. Zamaa said, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: There is no liability upon him. Sufyan said concerning a man in whose house there was a beast; he said: If there is a rider upon it or one holding it, and it strikes a man, he is liable; and if he tied it in a corner of the house and it strikes a man, there is no liability upon him; and if it was walking and kicked and struck a man, there is no liability upon him."
"17779","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If it kicks a man, there is no liability upon him, but he guarantees what it strikes with its foreleg. He said: And its explanation with us is when it was walking."
"17780","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi, he said: If a man ties his beast in the road of the Muslims, he guarantees what it strikes, and it is upon the clan."
"17781","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a man whose horse bolted with him and killed a man; he said: He is liable, in the position of one who shot an arrow at a bird and struck a man and killed him. He said: And Ibrahim said concerning a beast that struck with its leg then flailed with its foreleg: Half the blood-money, because the leg has no liability in it, while the foreleg is guaranteed, for we do not know whether it killed him with foreleg or leg. Muhammad ibn Jabir mentioned it, from Hammad, from Ibrahim."
"17782","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning the drunkard who kills or steals; he said: All the penalties are applied against him."
"17783","From al-Thawri, he said: Al-Shabi said: If the insane man is sane sometimes and insane sometimes, whatever he does in his lucidity, or if he slanders, the penalty is applied against him; and whatever he does while in frenzy, there is nothing upon him."
"17784","From Fudayl, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Whatever of it was in his state of lucidity is binding upon him."
"17785","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The sunna has passed that the deliberate act of the child and the insane is a mistake. Mamar said: And Qatada said it also."
"17786","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they both said: If the insane man does not reason and kills a man, the blood-money, for his deliberate act is a mistake; and if he reasons, retaliation."
"17787","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim said, concerning the insane man who throws at people and harms them: If they leave him free and release him, they bear what he causes; and if they bind and tie him, there is no compensation upon them."
"17788","From Ibrahim, from Husayn ibn Abd Allah, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, he said: The deliberate act of the child and the insane is a mistake."
"17789","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, concerning walls when leaning; he said: If they testify against him, he is liable. From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, the like of the saying of Shurayh: If the owner of the house sells his house, there is no liability upon the buyer unless they testify against him; and if they testify against the buyer, then the testified-against one says: I have rescinded for you, he has no right to rescind, for his testifying against him was for the Muslims generally; and there is nothing upon the seller in another's property, for it has come into another's ownership."
"17790","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning walls when leaning, that he should call witness against its owner, and if it falls upon a man and kills him; he said: The owner of the wall is liable."
"17791","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, he said: Shurayh held liable the open courtyard and the shades of the market people, when not in their ownership, and he held the pillar liable."
"17792","From al-Thawri, from Wasil, from al-Shabi, that Ali used to order the water-spouts and privies to be cut off from the road of the Muslims."
"17793","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever digs a well or lays a log and it strikes a man, he is liable."
"17794","Ibn Uyayna informed us, from Asim, from al-Shabi, he said: Shurayh had no spout except inside his house."
"17795","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: He used to hold the fuller liable if he poured water in the road and a man among market people or others slipped in it, when in other than his ownership."
"17796","From al-Thawri, he said: Ibrahim used to hold liable the beam protruding outward."
"17797","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith dug a well, and a mule fell into it while it was in the road, so they disputed to Shurayh, and he said: O Abu Umayya, is there liability for the well? He said: No, but upon Amr ibn al-Harith."
"17798","Mamar informed us, from Ashath, that two men dug a drain by their doors, and a man passed with his mule, and the foreleg of the mule fell into the drain and its leg broke. The people of the two houses came and he called them to witness against them, then went to Shurayh, who sent for them. A man said: O Shurayh, I am a poor man, and these two are notables. One of them said: I did not think a well imposed liability. Shurayh said: Yes, if you dug it in other than your own ground. He said: So they went to the side of the house and gave him the price of the mule. The names of the two men: al-Harith ibn Nawfal and al-Harith ibn Dirar."
"17799","From al-Thawri, he said: If you place your sandals or boots in a mosque and a man stumbles over them and is injured; he said: You are liable. He said: It is in the position of the road."
"17800","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever brings out anything beyond his boundary and it strikes a man, he is liable for it. From Ibn Uyayna, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, that he ruled likewise also."
"17801","From Hushaym, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Whoever digs in other than his building, or builds in other than his airspace, he is liable."
"17802","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning people who dug a well in the desert, and people passed it by night, and one of them fell into the well; he said: We hold nothing upon him. He compared that to the ruling of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning the mine and the well."
"17803","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning the vicious dog; he said: Its family bears what it strikes."
"17804","From Mamar, from Hammad, he said: They bear what it strikes outside their house."
"17805","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Harith informed me that a man of Hudhayl informed him that he heard Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As say: Concerning the hunting dog when killed: forty dirhams; and concerning the dog guarding crops and the house when it kills a sheep; and concerning the dog that barks but guards neither crops nor house: if its owner seeks it, a handful of dust. By Allah, we find this in the Book of Allah."
"17806","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, he said: Concerning the hunting dog: forty dirhams."
"17807","From al-Thawri, from Yala ibn Ata, from Ismail ibn Jastas, he said: I was with Abd Allah ibn Amr, and a man asked him: What is the compensation of the hunting dog? He said: Forty dirhams. He said: What is the compensation of the sheep dog? He said: A sheep from the sheep. He said: What is the compensation of the crop dog? He said: A measure of crops. He said: What is the compensation of the house dog? He said: A handful of dust; it is a duty upon the killer to pay it, and a duty upon its owner to accept it, though it diminishes the reward."
"17808","From Mamar, he said: It reached me concerning the hunting dog when killed; he said: He pays its owner the like of it."
"17809","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Shurayh ruled concerning the eye of the beast when gouged: a fourth of its price, if its owner had accepted its price, and if he wished, its purchase. Mamar said: And it reached me that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled likewise."
"17810","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, that Umar wrote to him: Concerning the eye of the beast, a fourth of its price."
"17811","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that a man informed him that Shurayh said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said to me: Concerning the eye of the beast, a fourth of its price."
"17812","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The eye of the beast? He said: The fourth, they claimed."
"17813","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, that Ali said: Concerning its eye, the fourth."
"17814","And I heard one narrating, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, that Umar ruled concerning a horse whose eye was struck: half its price."
"17815","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Mujalid, from al-Shabi, that Umar ruled concerning the eye of a camel that was struck: half its price, then he looked at it afterwards and said: I do not see it lacking anything in strength nor guidance, so he ruled therein a fourth of its price."
"17816","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata claimed to me that a freed stray camel among the strays of Mecca struck a man, and he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he said: You have nothing. He said: What if I wounded it? He said: Then I would take for him his due from you. He said: Will you not take for me from it? He said: No. He said: Then it is a viper. He said: If you leave me, I bite; and if you kill me, I take revenge. Umar said: Then it is a viper."
"17817","From Malik, from Abu al-Zinad, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that a freed slave, freed by one of the pilgrims, was playing with a man of Banu Aidh, and the freed slave killed the Aidhi. His father came to Umar ibn al-Khattab seeking his son's blood, but Umar refused to pay his blood-money; he said: He has no property. The Aidhi said: What if I had killed him? Umar said: Then you would pay his blood-money. He said: Then he is like a viper: if left, it bites, and if killed, it is avenged."
"17818","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said concerning the freed slave: The Muslims pay blood-money for him, and the Muslims inherit him; his former masters have nothing of him."
"17819","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: Every freed saiba, his former master pays blood-money for him and inherits him."
"17820","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me that Urwa informed him, from al-Harith al-Awar, that he asked Ali about a freed slave who killed a man deliberately; he said: He is killed for him, and if he killed by mistake, it is seen whether he contracted with anyone; if he contracted, his contractors are taken, and if he did not contract, it is paid for him from the treasury of the Muslims. And in the inheritance from him is an explanation."
"17821","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Crops struck by livestock by night or day? He said: Compensation is paid. I said: Is a fence incumbent upon him or not? He said: I hold compensation is paid. He said: I said: Was there one watching it? He said: So compensation is paid, in my view."
"17822","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is paid for crops? He said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say: Sulayman the Prophet, upon him be peace, ruled the shearing of the sheep, their milk, their offspring and their fat, all that for a year. I said to him: What is your firm position in that? He said: I do that; I repeated it to him, and he said: Glory be to Allah! The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled by it, as it reached us. I said to him: If a donkey ate it? He said: The value of what it ate."
"17823","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubruma, concerning crops when struck; he said: It is valued as it was when struck, valued in dirhams."
"17824","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Grazing by night and neglect by day? So Dawud ruled that they take the sheep themselves, but Allah made Sulayman understand it; when he was informed of the ruling of Dawud, he said: No, but take the sheep; yours is what comes from their milk, offspring and wool, until the year ends."
"17825","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Murra, from Masruq, concerning His saying: And Dawud and Sulayman, when they judged concerning the field when the sheep of the people grazed therein by night; he said: Their field was grapes, and the sheep grazed therein by night, so Dawud ruled the sheep for them. They passed by Sulayman and told him the news, and he said: Or other than that? So he sent them back to Dawud and said: What did you rule between them? He told him. He said: No, but rule between them that they take their sheep, and theirs be their milk, wool, ghee and benefit, and these tend their vineyard, until when it returns as it was, their sheep are returned to them. And that is His saying, mighty and majestic: So We made Sulayman understand it."
"17826","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, they both said: It reached us that their field was grapes."
"17827","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: They grazed therein, so Dawud gave them the sheep themselves for eating the field, while Sulayman ruled the shearing of the sheep and their milk for the field owners, with their pasturing incumbent upon the field owners, and the sheep owners cultivate, until it is as it was the day it was eaten, then they hand it to its owners and take their sheep."
"17828","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, and from all before them: That they report that the sheep grazed by night in the field in the time of Sulayman; and if they struck it by day, he did not impose payment."
"17829","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Muhayyisa, from his father, that a she-camel of al-Bara ibn Azib entered the garden of a man and spoiled in it. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled upon owners of property to guard it by day, and upon owners of livestock to guard it by night."
"17830","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said; Abu Umama ibn Sahl narrated to me that a she-camel entered the garden of some people and spoiled it, so the garden owners went to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Upon owners of property is guarding their property by day, and upon owners of livestock is guarding their livestock by night, and upon them is what it spoiled."
"17831","From Mamar, from Qatada, from al-Shabi, that a sheep fell into the yarn of a weaver, and they disputed to Shurayh. Al-Shabi said: Wait for him, for he will ask them: Did it fall by night or by day? He did so, then said: If by night, liability; and if by day, no liability. Then Shurayh recited: When the sheep of the people grazed therein by night. He said: Grazing is by night, and neglect is by day."
"17832","From al-Thawri, from a man, from a man, from al-Shabi, that a sheep fell into the yarn of a weaver and spoiled in it. He said: If by night, liability; and if by day, no liability. Then he recited: When the sheep of the people grazed therein by night. From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Shubruma, from al-Shabi, the like of it."
"17833","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, he said: Amir al-Shabi ruled concerning a sheep that entered upon householders; he said: If it entered by night, its owners pay; and if it entered by day, they do not pay."
"17834","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The fence is fastened and fenced upon the wall, yet it does not prevent the trained vicious one; is there anything in it? He said: No."
"17835","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab: He used to order the wall to be fortified and the fence fastened against the trained vicious one, then it is returned to its owners three times, then it is hamstrung."
"17836","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One who had looked into the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz during his caliphate to al-Hajjaj ibn Dhuayb informed me: that the wall should be fortified until it reaches the neck of the camel."
"17837","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say: The camel, or the cow, or the donkey, or the beasts of prey are to be sent back to their owners three times, when the wall has been fenced; then they are to be hamstrung."
"17838","From Mamar, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Said al-Sanani informed me that he heard Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, narrating, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lightest of the people of Hell in punishment is a man who steps on an ember so that his brain boils from it. He said: Then Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: And what was his crime, O Messenger of Allah? He said: He had livestock with which he would come upon crops and harm them, and Allah has made inviolable the crops and what is around them to the distance of an arrow shot, so beware lest a man prefer his wealth in this world and destroy himself in the Hereafter; so do not prefer your wealth in this world and destroy yourselves in the Hereafter."
"17839","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: Concerning a man killed deliberately, and his guardians say: We want the blood money, while the killer says: Kill me, he said: They have nothing but blood; if they wish they kill him, and if they wish they pardon, except that the killer wishes to give the blood money."
"17840","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: The killer is compelled to give the blood money. Allah Almighty said: Then whoever is pardoned something by his brother, then following with kindness; so pardon is to accept the blood money."
"17841","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Dinar, or Ibn Abi Najih, or both, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Among the Children of Israel there was retaliation, and there was no blood money among them; then Allah the Exalted said to this ummah: Retaliation is prescribed for you in cases of killing, the verse: Then whoever is pardoned something by his brother, he said: Pardon is that blood money be accepted in deliberate killing; then following with kindness: the claimant follows with kindness and the killer pays with excellence; that is a lightening from your Lord and a mercy, from what was decreed upon those before you. Ibn Uyaynah informed us of it, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas."
"17842","From Mamar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning a woman who killed a man: If the guardians wish to pardon, they pardon; and if they wish to kill, they kill; and if they wish to take the blood money, they take it; and they give his wife her inheritance from the blood money. He mentioned it from Simak."
"17843","From Muhammad ibn Yahya, from Ibn Harmalah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who is killed, his family has the better of two views: if they wish they take the blood money, and if they wish, killing."
"17844","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from al-Harith ibn al-Fadl, from Ibn Abi al-Awja al-Sulami, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever seeks blood or a wound - and khabl is a wound - he has a choice among three options; and if he wants a fourth, his hand is to be restrained, or he said: above his hand: between retaliating, or pardoning, or taking the blood money; then if he takes one of them and then transgresses after that, for him is Hell, abiding therein forever."
"17845","From Sufyan: Concerning a wound striking a man who is wounded, and the wounded man says: You hit me by mistake, while the other says: I hit him deliberately, he said: The proof is upon the wounded that it was a mistake, because he is claiming dirhams."
"17846","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh: That a slave wounded a group, so he ruled that he belongs to the other. He said: And we say: If the judgment has not taken place, he is shared equally among them. Hammad said it, and others among our companions."
"17847","From Sufyan: Concerning an umm walad who kills her master by mistake, he said: There is nothing upon her; but if she was mudabbara, she is sold for her value, because it is a bequest."
"17848","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever retracts his testimony after killing, upon him is the blood money according to his share. Mamar said: And al-Hasan used to say: Upon him is killing."
"17849","From Mamar, from Matar, from Ikrimah: Concerning four who testified against a man and a woman of adultery, so they were stoned, then one of them retracted, he said: Upon him is a quarter of the blood money from his own wealth."
"17850","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, that two men testified against a man before Ali that he stole, then they retracted their testimony, so he said: Had I known you did it deliberately, I would have cut off your hands; and he fined them the blood money for his hand."
"17851","Al-Thawri informed us, from Matar, from al-Shabi, that two men testified against a man of theft, so he cut him; then one of the two men came with a man and said: This is the one who stole; so Ali said: Had you two done it deliberately, I would have cut you both; so he invalidated their testimony against the other, and fined them the blood money of the first."
"17852","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, he said: Two men testified of theft against a man, so Ali cut off his hand; then the next day they came with a man and said: We erred regarding the first; it is this other one: so he invalidated their testimony against the other, and fined them the blood money of the first."
"17853","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning two men who testified against a man regarding a right, so judgment was passed against him, then they denied after that and said: We testified falsely, he said: If they were upright on the day they testified, their testimony stands. Mamar said, and al-Zuhri said, and Ibn Ulathah, the judge of the people of al-Jazirah: Their testimony is not valid, and the wealth is returned to the first."
"17854","From Mamar, and Ibn Shubrumah: Concerning two men who testified against a man of a right, so it was taken from him, then they said: We only testified forgery against him, he said: We fine them in their wealth."
"17855","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shubah, he said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad about two men who testified against a man of a right, so it was taken from him, then one of them retracted; so al-Hakam said: Their testimony stands; and Hammad said: This one who retracted guarantees his share."
"17856","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Shurayh, he said: A man testified before him with a testimony, so he carried out the judgment in it; then the man retracted afterwards, so he did not believe his statement."
"17857","From Hushaym, he said: Yazid ibn Zadawayh informed me that he heard al-Shabi being asked about a man against whom two men testified that he divorced his wife, so he separated them by their testimony; then one of the two witnesses married her after her waiting period ended; then he or the other retracted; so al-Shabi said: His retraction is not heeded when the judgment has passed."
"17858","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man testifies with two testimonies, the first is accepted and the other is left, and he is given the status of a boy. Abd al-Razzaq said: Sufyan said: We said: The witness is allowed to add to his testimony and reduce from it so long as the judgment has not passed; but when the judgment has passed and the witness retracts, he pays what he testified for. Sufyan said concerning a man who testified to the testimony of a man, so the judge judged by his testimony, then the witness about whose testimony he testified came and said: I did not make him witness of anything, he said: We say: When the judge has judged, the judgment stands."
"17859","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi: Concerning a man asked: Do you have testimony? So he says: No, then he testifies after that; so he allowed his testimony."
"17860","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One I trust informed me that: If four testify against a man of adultery and he is stoned, then they retract afterwards, then if they say: We did that deliberately, they are stoned; and if they say: We erred, it is rather so-and-so, they are not believed against so-and-so because of their first statement, and they are flogged for their other statement, and the blood money of the one stoned by their testimony is placed upon them in their wealth, and it is not placed upon the aqilah; and if three of them retract and say: We did that deliberately, they are killed, and the one who did not retract is neither lashed nor fined, and they are not believed against him; and likewise if one man or two men retract. He said: And likewise cutting and hadd in the hudud, when they testify against him then retract, then say: We did deliberately or we erred, like what I described concerning stoning; then if the four retract and say: We erred, it is rather so-and-so, they are flogged, and the blood money is placed upon them in their wealth specifically, and they are not believed against so-and-so."
"17861","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: The people of knowledge told me: If two men testify against a man that he owes a right to so-and-so, so it is taken from him, then they say: It is really upon so-and-so, and they were upright the first time, he said: The wealth is taken from them both; if they say: We did it deliberately with that testimony on purpose, or we erred, then the wealth is taken from them and paid to the one against whom they first testified."
"17862","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: The blood money of a woman of the People of the Book is four thousand dirhams. He said: I said: And the Arab Christians? He said: Like them."
"17863","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) imposed upon every Muslim man who kills a man of the People of the Book four thousand dirhams, and that he is to be exiled from his land to another. And that a man of Khatham killed a man of the people of al-Harrah at the time of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and that Umar exiled him from the land of Khatham, or he said: from his house. Amr said: So he stayed with us until we equipped him to his people, and he departed."
"17864","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the blood money of the People of the Book, of the Jews and Christians, half the blood money of the Muslim."
"17865","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a shaykh, from Umar: That a man was brought to him who had killed a Jew or a Christian, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj."
"17866","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and from Amr, from al-Hasan, they both said: The blood money of the Jew and the Christian is four thousand."
"17867","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and others, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz made the blood money of the Jew and the Christian half the blood money of the Muslim."
"17868","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Miqdam, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab made the blood money of the Jew and the Christian four thousand dirhams."
"17869","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from a man, that Abu Musa wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab concerning a Muslim man who killed a man of the People of the Book, so Umar wrote to him: If he was a thief or a warrior, strike his neck; and if it was a sudden outburst from him in anger, fine him four thousand dirhams."
"17870","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, he said: I heard Abu Malih ibn Usamah narrating that a Muslim killed a man of the people of Kufa, so Abu Musa wrote concerning him to Umar, so Umar wrote concerning him: If it was a sudden outburst from him, fine him the blood money; and if it was character or habit, retaliate against him from him."
"17871","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled concerning a man who killed a man of the dhimmah, a Christian or a Jew, so he wrote: If he was a habitual thieving aggressor, kill him; and if it was only a sudden outburst from him over an insult, fine him four thousand dirhams."
"17872","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The blood money of the Magian? He said: Eight hundred dirhams."
"17873","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me that Abu Musa al-Ashari wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab that the Muslims fall upon the Magians and kill them, so what is your view? So Umar wrote to him: They are but slaves, so value them at the value of a slave among you. So Abu Musa wrote of eight hundred dirhams, and Umar established it for the Magian."
"17874","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The blood money of the Magian is eight hundred dirhams. From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, like the saying of Ibn al-Musayyib."
"17875","From Mamar, from Simak and others, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz made the blood money of the Magian half the blood money of the Muslim."
"17876","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The blood money of the dhimmi is five hundred dinars."
"17877","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Sulayman ibn Said, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab made the blood money of the Magian eight hundred dirhams."
"17878","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah, from Makhul, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled with eight hundred dirhams."
"17879","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The blood money of the Jew, the Christian, the Magian, and every dhimmi is like the blood money of the Muslim. He said: And so it was at the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, until Muawiyah came and put half of it in the treasury and gave the family of the slain half. Then Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled with half the blood money, and abolished what Muawiyah had put in the treasury. He said: And I think Umar saw that half which Muawiyah had put in the treasury as injustice from him. Al-Zuhri said: So I did not get the chance to discuss it with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and inform him that the blood money had been complete for the people of dhimmah. I said to al-Zuhri: It has reached me that Ibn al-Musayyib said: His blood money is four thousand. So he said: The best of matters is what is presented to the Book of Allah; Allah the Exalted said: Blood money delivered to his family; so when you have given him a third of the blood money, you have delivered it to him."
"17880","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: That a Muslim man killed a man of the people of dhimmah deliberately, so he was brought to Uthman, who did not kill him for him, and made the blood money heavy upon him like the blood money of the Muslim. Al-Zuhri said: And Khalid ibn al-Muhajir killed a man of the people of dhimmah in the time of Muawiyah, who did not kill him for him, and made the blood money heavy upon him, a thousand dinars. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, of Uthman and Muawiyah, the like of it."
"17881","From Abu Hanifah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, that Ali said: The blood money of the Jew, the Christian, and every dhimmi is like the blood money of the Muslim. Abu Hanifah said: And it is my saying."
"17882","From Rabah ibn Abdullah, he said: Humayd al-Tawil informed me that he heard Anas narrating: That a Jewish man was killed treacherously, so Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled in his case with twelve thousand dirhams."
"17883","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Masud, he said: The blood money of the muahid is like the blood money of the Muslim. And Ali said that also."
"17884","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, transmitting from Ibn Masud, that he said: Concerning every muahid, Magian or other, the blood money is complete."
"17885","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yaqub ibn Utbah, and Salih, and Ismail ibn Muhammad, they said: The blood money of every muahid among the people of disbelief, male and female, is like the blood money of the Muslims; the Sunnah ran with that in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"17886","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The blood money of the Jew, the Christian, and the Magian is like the blood money of the Muslim. Mamar said: And al-Shabi said it also."
"17887","From Mamar, and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The blood money of the dhimmi is the blood money of the Muslim."
"17888","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Shabi, he said: The blood money of the Jew and the Christian is the blood money of the Muslim, and his expiation is like the expiation of the Muslim."
"17889","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no retaliation upon a Muslim for a disbeliever. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote in the document which he wrote between Quraysh and the Ansar: That no believer is to be killed for a disbeliever. Mamar said: Al-Zuhri informed me of it."
"17890","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah: Concerning a Muslim killing a dhimmi, he said: In it is the blood money, and there is no retaliation upon him. And al-Thawri said it, from Simak, from Ikrimah."
"17891","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled that no Muslim is to be killed for a disbeliever."
"17892","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, he said: A Muslim is not retaliated for a dhimmi, nor for a slave."
"17893","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Qazah informed me, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Muslims are one hand against others; their blood is equal, and the least of them grants protection; and no Muslim is to be killed for a disbeliever, nor one under covenant while in his covenant."
"17894","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: It was said to Ali: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrust anything to you? He said: No, except what is in this bag; so he brought out a sheet from the bag, and in it was: The believers are one hand against others; their blood is equal, and the least of them grants protection; no believer is to be killed for a disbeliever, nor one under covenant while in his covenant."
"17895","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, from Abu Juhayfah, he said: I said to Ali: Do you have anything besides the Quran? He said: No, by the One who split the seed and created the soul, except that Allah gives a servant understanding in His Book, or what is in the sheet. He said: I said: And what is in the sheet? He said: The blood money, and freeing the captive, and no Muslim is to be killed for a disbeliever."
"17896","From Mamar, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab came to Sham and found a man of the Muslims who had killed a man of the people of dhimmah; so he was about to retaliate against him, so Zayd ibn Thabit said to him: Would you retaliate your slave for your brother? So Umar made his blood money."
"17897","From al-Thawri, from Humayd, from Makhul, that Umar wanted to retaliate against a Muslim man for a man of the people of dhimmah in a wound, so Zayd ibn Thabit said to him: Would you retaliate your slave for your brother?"
"17898","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Husayn informed me that a Muslim man gashed a man of the people of dhimmah; so Umar ibn al-Khattab was about to retaliate against him. Muadh ibn Jabal said: You have known that that is not for him, and he traced that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so Umar ibn al-Khattab gave him a dinar for his gash, and he was pleased with it."
"17899","From Mamar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: The wounds of a man of the people of dhimmah are half the wounds of the Muslim."
"17900","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A Muslim kills a Christian deliberately, he said: His blood money. He said: I said: Is it made heavy upon him in the sanctuary? He said: No."
"17901","From al-Thawri, from Rabiah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he retaliated against a Muslim who killed a Jew, and said: I am the most deserving to fulfill my covenant."
"17902","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: That a Muslim man killed a man of the people of dhimmah of the people of al-Hirah, so Umar retaliated against him from him."
"17903","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: That he used to hold the retaliation of the Muslim for the dhimmi. From Abu Hanifah, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"17904","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Maymun ibn Mihran, he said: I witnessed the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz come to the commander of al-Jazirah, or he said: al-Hirah: Concerning a Muslim man who killed a man of the people of dhimmah: Hand him to his guardian; if he wishes he kills him, and if he wishes he pardons him. He said: So he was handed to him, and he struck his neck, while I was looking."
"17905","Mamar said, from Simak ibn al-Fadl: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote, concerning Ziyad ibn Muslim who killed an Indian in Aden: That fine him five hundred dinars, and do not kill him."
"17906","From Mamar, from Layth, I think from al-Shabi, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: Concerning a man of the people of al-Jazirah, a Christian, killed by a Muslim, that his companion be retaliated; so they kept saying to the Christian: Kill him. He said: No, he refused until the kin come; and while he was so, the letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab came: Do not retaliate him from him."
"17907","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hakam, from al-Ashath, from al-Ijli, from Abu Bakrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a covenanted soul without its lawful due, Paradise is forbidden for him to smell its scent, though its scent is found from a distance of a hundred years. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"17908","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A Christian kills a Muslim deliberately, but he had no knowledge of it by him."
"17909","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: The Muslim is given choice; if he wishes, retaliation, and if he wishes, the blood money."
"17910","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, that a man of the Jews killed a girl of the Ansar for her jewelry, then threw her in a well and smashed her head with stones; so he was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): and he ordered concerning him that he be stoned until he dies, so he was stoned until he died."
"17911","From Mamar, and he was asked about a Christian who killed a Muslim slave, he said: He is handed to the master of the slave; if he wishes he kills him. Allah the Exalted said: And a believing slave is better than a polytheist."
"17912","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar said to me: Remember three from me: Command is consultation, and in the ransom of the Arabs, in place of every slave a slave, and concerning the son of the slave woman, two slaves. And Ibn Tawus concealed the third."
"17913","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled concerning the ransom of the Arabs with six obligations."
"17914","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Uthman ruled in place of every slave, and in place of every slave girl, two slave girls."
"17915","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled concerning the ransom of Arab slaves from themselves: So he ruled concerning the man taken captive in the Jahiliyyah with eight camels, and concerning a child, if he had one from a slave woman, with two servants each, every person among them male or female, and he ruled concerning a Jahiliyyah captive woman with ten camels, and he ruled concerning her child from a slave with two servants, and his blood money belongs to the masters of his mother, and they are her kin, then to them belongs his inheritance and her inheritance so long as his father is not freed, and he ruled concerning an Islamic captive with six camels for the man, the woman, and the child, and that is among the Arabs. He said: And I heard: that their saying concerning the child of the slave woman, the umm walad of a Muslim, is when the people of Islam take captive the people of apostasy."
"17916","From Mamar, from a man who heard Ikrimah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled concerning the ransom of Arab slaves from themselves: Concerning the man taken captive in the Jahiliyyah with eight camels, and concerning a child, if he had one from a slave woman, with two servants each, every person among them male or female, and he ruled concerning a Jahiliyyah captive woman with ten camels, and he ruled concerning her child from a slave with two servants, and his blood money belongs to the masters of his mother, and they are her kin, and to them belongs his inheritance so long as his father is not freed, and he ruled concerning an Islamic captive with six camels, for the man, the woman, and the child."
"17917","From Mamar, from al-Hasan, he said: In place of every slave, a slave."
"17918","From Mamar, from more than one, that the people of Oman were taken captive: So Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled concerning them with four hundred dirhams; then he looked afterwards and said: They were only taken captive in Islam, so they are free wherever you find them."
"17919","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Muslim informed me that Tawus narrated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled concerning Arab captives: Concerning the clients with two slaves or eight camels, and concerning the Arab with a slave or four camels. Amr said: The Arab captives are those whom the people accepted Islam while they were in their hands."
"17920","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from al-Zuhri, he said: A woman does not retaliate against her husband. Sufyan said: And we say: She retaliates against him except in discipline."
"17921","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah informed me, from Amr ibn Muslim, their freedman, and Ibn al-Musayyib was asked about a man who beats his wife, or his hired man, or his boy, or the ruler in his rule, he said: There is no blood money in that nor retaliation, little the beating or much, when it is according to the fault, except that he transgresses beyond the measure of the punishment of the fault, then his hand is restrained, so the blood money becomes due, by the guardians of the slain swearing fifty oaths: he died from the excess which he added beyond the measure of his fault."
"17922","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Waging war is shirk. And Abd al-Karim, and I say: We do not know that anyone wages war against the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except he associated partners."
"17923","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, that a group of Ukl and Uraynah spoke about Islam, so they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): and they informed him that they were people of udders, and were not people of countryside, so they found Madinah unhealthy, and complained of its fever: so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered for them some milch camels, and ordered a herdsman for them, and ordered them to go out of Madinah, to drink from their milk and urine; so they went until, when they were at al-Harrah, they disbelieved after their Islam, and killed the herdsman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and drove the camels; so that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent seekers after them, so they were brought, so he blinded their eyes, and cut off their hands and feet, and they were left at al-Harrah biting its stones until they died. Qatadah said: It reached us that this verse was revealed concerning them: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger, the whole verse."
"17924","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made an example of those who stole his milch camels, so he cut off their hands and feet, and blinded their eyes."
"17925","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me that he heard Said ibn Jubayr informing that people of Banu Sulaym came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: O Messenger of Allah! We have accepted Islam, but we find Madinah unhealthy. He said: So be with my milch camels; they come to you morning and evening, and you drink from their milk. So they killed its herdsman and drove them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made an example of them; then was revealed: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger, the verse."
"17926","From Ibrahim, from Salih, the freedman of al-Tawamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Men of Banu Fazarah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wasted and lean: so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to his milch camels, to drink from them until they recovered; then they came upon his milch camels and stole them, so they were sought, so they were brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he cut off their hands and feet, and blinded their eyes. Abu Hurayrah said: So this verse was revealed concerning them: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left blinding of eyes afterwards."
"17927","From Mamar, from Qatadah, and Ata al-Khurasani, and al-Kalbi, they said: Concerning this verse: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger, they said: This is concerning the thief who cuts the road, for he is a warrior; then if he kills and takes wealth, he is crucified; and if he kills and does not take wealth, he is killed; and if he takes wealth and does not kill, his hand and foot are cut; then if he is taken before he does anything of that, he is exiled. They said: And as for His saying: Except those who repent before you overpower them: this is for the people of shirk; whoever of the polytheists afflicts something of the Muslims while he is at war with them, taking wealth or shedding blood, then repents before he is overpowered, what passed is dropped from him."
"17928","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, or another, that Said ibn Jubayr said: Whoever wages war, he is a warrior; then if he sheds blood, he is killed; and if he sheds blood and takes wealth, he is crucified; and if he takes wealth and does not shed blood, his hand and foot are cut from opposite sides; then if he repents, his repentance is between him and Allah, and the hadd is carried out upon him."
"17929","From Ibrahim, from Dawud, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: This verse was revealed concerning the warrior: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger; when he aggresses and cuts the road, killing and taking wealth, he is crucified; and if he kills and does not take wealth, he is killed; and if he takes wealth and does not kill, he is cut from opposite sides; then if he flees and escapes them, that is his exile."
"17930","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: Concerning one who wages war, that upon him is to be killed, or crucified, or cut, or exiled, so he is not overpowered; whichever of that the Imam wishes, he does with him; then whenever he is overpowered, one of these hudud is carried out upon him. He said: If he frightens the road and does not take wealth, he is exiled; and his exile is that he be sought and not overpowered; whenever he is heard of in a land, he is sought."
"17931","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, or another, he said: I heard Said ibn Jubayr and Abu al-Shatha saying: Exile is only that they not be caught; then if they are caught, in them is the judgment of Allah; otherwise they are exiled until they join their land."
"17932","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a man who commits an offense in Islam then joins the abode of war, then the Imam overpowers him after that, he said: If he apostatized from Islam as a disbeliever, what he committed is warded off from him; and if he did not apostatize, what he committed is carried out upon him."
"17933","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, concerning one who steals stealthily and commits hudud, then comes repentant, he said: If that were accepted from them, they would dare upon it, and many people would do it; but had he fled to the enemy, then came repentant, I would not see punishment upon him."
"17934","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If they affirm Islam, then wage war, shedding blood and taking wealth, then are taken, then in them is the judgment of Allah, and they are not pardoned, and retaliation is taken from them for what they committed. And Abd al-Karim said: Ata said: Whichever of that the Imam wishes, he judges in them; if he wishes he kills them, or crucifies them, or cuts off their hands and feet from opposite sides; if the Imam wishes he does one of them and leaves the rest."
"17935","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If they affirm Islam, then wage war, but draw near neither blood nor wealth, until they repent before they are overpowered, then there is no way against them. And Abd al-Karim said that."
"17936","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: Concerning the thief who repents, he said: There is no cutting upon a repentant."
"17937","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, or another, from al-Hasan, he said: Whoever wages war, he is a warrior."
"17938","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The punishment of the warrior is to the ruler; the pardon of the guardian of blood is not allowed; that is to the Imam."
"17939","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa said to me: The guardian of blood pardons if he wishes, or takes the blood money when they settle; and the ruler is the guardian of one who wages war against the religion; then if he kills the brother of a man or his father, there is nothing to the seeker of blood of the affair of one who wages war against the religion and strives in the earth in corruption."
"17940","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that in a letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab: And the ruler is the guardian of one who wages war against the religion; and if they kill his father or his brother, there is nothing to the seeker of blood of the affair of one who wages war against the religion and strives in the earth in corruption."
"17941","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The thief, when is fighting him lawful for me? He said: When they frighten security, cut the road, and fight; then if they are taken after having fought, none of them is killed except one who killed, and wealth is taken from whoever took it among them, and there is no cutting. He said: And I say: He is a warrior; in him is what Said ibn Jubayr said."
"17942","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: Ibn Umar seized a thief in his house: so she drew the sword against him; and had we not forbidden him from him, he would have struck him with it."
"17943","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, he said: I said to him: What if a man enters my house? He said: He who enters your house, what Allah has made unlawful is not lawful for you from him, but his self is lawful for you."
"17944","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The thief is a warrior against Allah and His Messenger, so kill him; and whatever befalls you in him of anything, it is upon me."
"17945","From Mamar, from a man to whom thieves presented themselves, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me: He saw no harm in fighting them."
"17946","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked him about a man confronting a man wanting his wealth, should he fight him? Ibrahim said: Had he left him, I would have hated him."
"17947","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Hasan, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Talhah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has his wealth sought unjustly, and fights, and is killed, he is a martyr."
"17948","From al-Aslami, from Sulayman, from Asim, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever apostatizes from his religion, kill him."
"17949","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Sahl, from Said ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever steals a span of land, he is collared with it from seven earths. Mamar said: And it reached me from him that he said: And whoever is killed defending his wealth, he is a martyr."
"17950","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Shihab, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, from Said ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is killed defending his wealth, he is a martyr."
"17951","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Muawiyah sent to an agent of his to take al-Waht; so that reached Abdullah ibn Amr, so he wore his weapon, he and his freedmen and his boys, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever is killed defending his wealth wronged, he is a martyr. So the commander wrote to Muawiyah that he had prepared for fighting, and said he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever is killed defending his wealth, he is a martyr. So Muawiyah wrote: Leave him and his wealth."
"17952","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As prepared for fighting defending al-Waht. He said: Why should I not fight defending it, when I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever is killed defending his wealth, he is a martyr. I said to him: Who wanted to fight? He said: Anbasah ibn Abi Sufyan."
"17953","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me that Thabit, the freedman of Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman, informed him, he said: When what happened between Abdullah ibn Amr and Anbasah ibn Abi Sufyan happened, and they prepared for fighting, Khalid ibn al-As rode to Abdullah ibn Amr and admonished him; so Abdullah said: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is killed for his wealth, he is a martyr."
"17954","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz in it: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is killed defending his wealth, he is a martyr."
"17955","From al-Aslami, from a man, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fights defending himself until he is killed, he is a martyr; and whoever fights defending his family until he is killed, he is a martyr; and whoever is killed in the love of Allah, he is a martyr."
"17956","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: I know only that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a man is killed defending his wealth, he is a martyr."
"17957","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn Mukhariq, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah! If a man comes to me seizing my goods? He said: Remind him of Allah. He said: And if I remind him of Allah and he does not remember? He said: Seek help against him from the Muslims present with you. He said: And if none are present with me and he wants my goods? He said: Go to the ruler. He said: What if the ruler refuses me? He said: Fight him until you are written among the martyrs of the Hereafter, or you keep what is yours."
"17958","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is lawful for me of fighting the Haruriyyah? He said: When they cut the road and frighten security."
"17959","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, he said: The Haruriyyah went out and disputed with Ali and left him, and testified against him of shirk, but he did not provoke them; then they went out to Harura, so he was brought news that they were equipping from Kufa, so he said: Leave them; then they went out and camped at Nahrawan, and stayed a month, so it was said to him: Raid them now. So he said: Not until they shed blood, cut the road, and frighten security. So he did not provoke them until they killed, then he raided them, and they were killed. He said: So I said to him: A Kharijite band that went out from the Muslims, they did not associate partners, so they were taken and drew near nothing, are they killed? He said: No."
"17960","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, he said: They are not killed. He said: Ali ibn Abi Talib was brought a man who had slung his sword and worn his cloak over it, and wanted to kill him; so he said to him: You wanted to kill me? He said: Yes. He said: Why? He said: For what you know in myself towards you. So they said: Kill him. He said: Rather leave him; then if he kills me, kill him."
"17961","From al-Thawri, from Isa ibn al-Mughirah, he said: A Kharijite went out with the sword in Khurasan and was seized, so writing was made concerning him to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, so he wrote concerning him: If he wounded anyone, wound him; and if he killed anyone, kill him; otherwise lodge him in prison, and place his family near him, until he repents from the evil view."
"17962","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal al-Adawi, he said: Ali did not deem fighting the Haruriyyah lawful until they killed Ibn Khabbab."
"17963","From Mamar, he said: More than one of Abd al-Qays informed me, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from his father, he said: I had come to the Khawarij, and they were the most beloved people on the face of the earth to me; so I stayed among them until they differed. So it was said to Ali: Fight them. So he said: No, until they kill. Then a man passed by them, and they disapproved his appearance, so they went to him, and he was Abdullah ibn Khabbab. So they said: Narrate to us what you heard your father narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I heard him saying: He heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There will be a trial; the sitter in it is better than the stander, and the stander is better than the walker, and the walker is better than the runner, and the runner is in the Fire. He said: So they took him and his slave mother, and slaughtered them both together in the Fire on the bank of the river. He said: And I saw their blood in the river as if two straps. So Ali was informed of that, so he said to them: Retaliate me for Ibn Khabbab. They said: All of us killed him. Then he deemed fighting them lawful."
"17964","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A man asked him, I think of the people of Yamamah, he said: We came to the Haruriyyah at such and such time; they asked us about nothing, except that they kill whoever they meet. So Ibn Sirin said: I knew none who scrupled from killing these out of sin, nor from killing one who wants your wealth, except the ruler, for the ruler has a right."
"17965","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: When the Haruriyyah came to us, my father fled and joined Makkah; then he met Ibn Umar and said: The Haruriyyah came to us, so I fled from them; and had they caught me, they would have killed me. So Ibn Umar said: You succeeded then and prospered. So he said to him: What if I sit and pledge them when I fear trial upon me, for a man is tried in what is easier than this."
"17966","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: My father used to urge on the day of Ruzayq in fighting the Haruriyyah. He said: And I mentioned the Khawarij before Ibn Amir, so he mentioned of their striving, so he said: They are not more striving than the Jews and Christians, yet they are killed."
"17967","My father informed me, he said: When Najdah came to Sana, Wahb entered the mosque and called the people to fighting them; and while they were pledging him, his father was informed of that, so he came and prevented him."
"17968","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar informed me that Najdah met him and loosened the fastening of his sword and let it loose. He said: Then he passed by him and loosened it also and let it loose; then he passed by him the third time and said: Who let this loose? As if there is not in yourselves what is in ourselves."
"17969","From Mamar, he said: Al-Zuhri informed me that Sulayman ibn Hisham wrote to him asking him about a woman who went out from her husband, testified against her people of shirk, and joined the Haruriyyah, and married, then she returned to her family repentant. Al-Zuhri said: So I wrote to him: As for after, the first trial broke out while of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who witnessed Badr there were many; so their opinion gathered that they would not carry out upon anyone a hadd in a private part deemed lawful by interpretation of the Quran, nor retaliation in killing inflicted upon interpretation of the Quran, nor return what they took upon interpretation of the Quran, except that it be found itself, so it is returned to its owner; and I see that she be returned to her husband, and that whoever slandered her be hadd-punished."
"17970","From Mamar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl and others, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning wealth which Ibn Yusuf had taken from people: Whatever is found itself, return it to its owner."
"17971","From Mamar, from a man of Anazah called Sayf ibn so-and-so ibn Muawiyah, he said: My maternal uncle narrated to me, from my grandfather, he said: When the day of the Camel came and the horses became disordered, the people came to Ali claiming things, and spoke much to him. So he said: Is there none among you who gathers his speech for me in five words or six until I understand what he says? He said: So I sprang upon one of my legs and said: Shall I speak; if my speech pleases him, else I sit. So I said: O Commander of the Believers! Speech is not in five nor six, but it is two words. He said: So he looked at me, so I said: Withholding or retaliation. He said with his hand, and contracted thirty: They said so. Then he said: Have you seen everything you contract, it is under this foot of mine. And he says to him of his legs."
"17972","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr informed me, from Amr ibn Sulaym, that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: When the two parties meet, whatever blood or wound is between them is void. Do you not hear the saying of Allah Almighty: And if two parties of the believers fight, so he recited the verse until he finished from it. He said: So each of the two parties sees the other as rebellious."
"17973","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq, from Arfajah, from his father, that Ali recognized the remnants of the people of al-Nahr; so the last that remained was a pot which he recognized, but it was not recognized."
"17974","From Ibn Uyaynah, from their companions, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Ismah al-Asadi, he said: The people rushed to Ali and said: Divide among us their women and offspring. So Ali said: The men wronged me, so I wronged them; and these are offspring of a Muslim people in an abode of migration, and you have no way to them; whatever the houses shelter of their wealth, it is theirs; and whatever they brought against you in your camp, it is booty for you."
"17975","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, that he heard him saying: Ali ibn Abi Talib said: No finishing off the wounded, no killing a captive, no pursuing one who turns back; and he would not take wealth of a slain, saying: Whoever acknowledges something, let him take it."
"17976","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Juwaybir, he said: A woman of Banu Asad informed me, she said: I heard Ammar, after Ali had finished from the companions of the Camel, calling: Do not kill one advancing, nor one turning back, nor finish off a wounded, nor enter a house; whoever lays down arms, he is safe; and whoever closes his door, he is safe."
"17977","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Fakhitah, he said: A neighbor of mine narrated to me, he said: I brought Ali a captive on the day of Siffin, so he said to me: Release him; I will not kill him in bonds; I fear Allah, Lord of the worlds. Is there good in you? Pledge. And he said to the one who brought him: To you is his spoil."
"17978","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Asim al-Thaqafi, from shaykhs of his people, they said: We heard Ali saying: Have you seen, had I been absent from the people, who would go among them with this conduct?"
"17979","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When Ali finished from fighting the companions of the Camel, a man stood and said: The blood of the people of Basra became lawful for us, and their wealth and women became unlawful for us? So Ali said: Silence this, until he said it two or three times. So Ali stood to him: Do you want the opinion of learners? So the people said: Who is this learner? He said: So the man went."
"17980","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When Ali saw Ibn Muljim, he would say: I want his life, and he wants my killing; excuse me from your friend of Murad."
"17981","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulayka informed me, from Uqba ibn al-Harith who informed him, that Fayruz Abu Musa came forward with two slaves belonging to Abd Allah ibn Abi Salama, and Fayruz himself also belonged to Abd Allah ibn Abi Salama, then the two slaves killed Fayruz, so Marwan killed them. He said: Abu Husayn ibn al-Harith wrote to me asking me to speak to him, for they were two slaves of ours who killed our slave, and he should not have killed them. He said: I have sought good reward in killing them. He said: then compensate us for them. Uqba said: so I spoke to Marwan but he refused, and I said: if he comes to Mecca he will surely compensate Abu Husayn. He said: then he came to Mecca and gave him their value, two hundred dinars. And Ibn Abi Mulayka said: Ibn Alqama Ribh, belonging to Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Umayya, killed a slave-lad belonging to Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad, so Nafi ibn Alqama killed them, then he was told of Marwan's compensation for the two lads of his brother's sons, so he wrote to Abd al-Malik: see what Marwan did and do it, supporting him. He said: so he did, and Abd al-Malik compensated for his lads."
"17982","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, he said: we read to Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me of a report he raised to Abd Allah ibn Amr. Abd al-Razzaq said: as for al-Muthanna, he informed us from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that al-Muzani asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, what about the stray sheep? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take it, for it is only for you, or for your brother, or for the wolf, so take it until its seeker comes."
"17983","He said: O Messenger of Allah, what about the stray camel? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With it are its water-skin and its sandals, and it grazes on the land, and the wolf is not feared for it, so leave it until its seeker comes."
"17984","He said: O Messenger of Allah, what about wealth that is found? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever is on a frequented road or in an inhabited village, announce it for a year; if its seeker comes, return it to him, and if you do not find a seeker, it is yours; then if a seeker comes one day, return it to him."
"17985","He said: O Messenger of Allah, what about what is found in a ruined village? He said: In it and in buried treasure is the fifth."
"17986","He said: O Messenger of Allah, what about the pastured flock on the mountain that is stolen? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In it is its fine and the like of it with it, and exemplary lashes."
"17987","He said: O Messenger of Allah, what about fruit hanging on the tree? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Its fine and the like of it with it, and exemplary lashes."
"17988","He said: O Messenger of Allah, what about what the threshing floor and the resting pen enclose? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever reaches the price of a shield, the hand of its taker is cut, and the price of a shield was ten dirhams; whatever is less than that, its fine and the like of it, and exemplary lashes. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pardon one another among yourselves before you come to me, for whatever hadd reaches me has become due."
"17989","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about one whose camels are stolen while grazing, he said: The fine is doubled against him also, and he is punished exemplarily likewise."
"17990","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Ikrima, I think from Abu Hurayra: its fine and the like of it with it."
"17991","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The concealed stray, meaning camels, stays with its mate."
"17992","From Ma'mar, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Aqil ibn Abi Talib, from Khalid ibn Zayd, from his father Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the stray of the sheep's shepherd. He said: It is for you, or for your brother, or for the wolf. He said: and another said: for your brother."
"17993","He said: What do you say, O Messenger of Allah, about the stray camel? He said: What have you to do with it? With it are its water-skin and its sandals, and it eats from the ends of the trees. Ma'mar said: and I heard another say: perhaps it remembers its homeland and returns. Then he returned to the hadith."
"17994","And he said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about silver coins if I find them? He said: Note its container, its tie, and its number, then announce it for a year; if its owner comes, hand it to him, otherwise it is yours, enjoy it, or the like of this."
"17995","From al-Thawri, from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munba'ith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, he said: A bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him about the lost object. He said: Announce it for a year, then know its container and its tie, or he said: its receptacle; if its owner comes, hand it to him, otherwise spend it or enjoy it."
"17996","He said: O Messenger of Allah, what about the stray sheep? He said: It is only for you, or for your brother, or for the wolf."
"17997","He said: then he asked him about the stray camel, and the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed, and he said: What have you to do with it? With it are its sandals and its water-skin; it comes to water and eats trees; leave it until its lord finds it."
"17998","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from al-Jarud al-Abdi, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The stray of the Muslim is a brand of the Fire, so do not approach it. He said: we think it means camels; al-Thawri is the one who said it."
"17999","From Ibn Uyayna, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: People came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking mounts, and found none with him. They said: Do you permit us stray camels? He said: That is a brand of the Fire."
"18000","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abu Qaza'a claim that al-Jarud, when he accepted Islam, said: O Messenger of Allah, what about camels we find between us and our families to ride upon? He said: That is a brand of the Fire."
"18001","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abu Qaza'a claim that al-Jarud reported that four men of Bani Amir ibn Lu'ayy attacked a camel they saw and slaughtered it, and Umar was approached about it, and with him was Hatib ibn Abi Balta'a, brother of Bani Amir. He said: O Hatib, go now and buy for the camel's owner two camels for his camel. So Hatib did, and they were lashed and released."
"18002","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar wrote to his agents: Do not let strays go astray. Camels would breed freely and come to waters with none touching them, until one who recognized them came and took them. Then when it was Uthman's time, he wrote: gather them and announce them; if one who knows them comes, otherwise sell them and place their prices in the treasury; if one who recognizes them comes, hand the prices to him."
"18003","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr claim that a man in Umar ibn al-Khattab's time found a stray camel and brought it to Umar. Umar said: Announce it for a month. He did, then came to him with it. Umar said: Add a month. He did, then came to him. He said to him: Add a month. He did, then came to him and said: We have branded it; it has eaten our water-camel's fodder. Umar said: What have you to do with it! Where did you find it? He told him. He said: Go and send it where you found it."
"18004","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: Thabit ibn al-Dahhak informed me, he said: I found a camel in Umar's time and brought it to Umar. He said: Announce it. I said: I have announced it until it has distracted me from my slaves and tending my land. He said: Send it where you found it. From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Sa'id and Ayyub ibn Abi Tamima, that they heard Sulayman ibn Yasar say: Thabit ibn al-Dahhak al-Ansari informed me, like the hadith of Ma'mar."
"18005","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: None gathers strays but a stray."
"18006","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib say: Umar ibn al-Khattab said, leaning his back on the Ka'ba: Whoever takes a stray is a stray. Yahya said: we think it means camels."
"18007","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that Ali said: None consumes the stray but a stray. From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id, from Qatada, from Khilas ibn Amr, from Ali, the like of it."
"18008","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, he said: I went out with Zayd ibn Suhan and Salman ibn Rabi'a al-Bahili, and I picked up a whip at al-Udhayb. They said to me: Leave it! I said: By Allah, I will not leave it for beasts to eat; I will surely enjoy it. So I came to Ubayy ibn Ka'b and told him. He said: You did well, you did well. I found a purse in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) containing a hundred dinars, and I brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him. He said: Announce it for a year. So I announced it for a year, then came to him, and he said: Announce it for a year. So I announced it for a year, then came to him, and he said: Announce it for a year. I said: I have kept coming to it announcing it. He said: so I announced it for three years, then came to him after three years, and he said: Note its number and its tie; if one comes informing you of its number, its container, and its tie, hand it to him, otherwise enjoy it."
"18009","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, about the lost object, he said: It is Allah's wealth which He gives to whom He wills."
"18010","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Hasan: Take it. And he said: While we were on the night of Muzdalifa under Uthman's rule, a woman pilgrim came with her wrap and placed it on some of our baggage, then missed us, and we knew not whose it was. So we announced it for a year, then men of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, and we told them we had announced it for a year, and they said: Enjoy it."
"18011","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi found a chest containing great wealth and brought it to Umar ibn al-Khattab, telling him of it. Umar said: It is yours. He said: O Commander of the Believers, I have no need of it; another is needier than I. He said: Announce it for a year. He did, then came to him with it. Umar said: It is yours. He said the like of his first saying. Umar said: Announce it for a year. He did, then came to him with it. Umar said: It is yours. Sufyan said the like of his first saying. Umar said: Announce it for a year. He did, then came to him with it. Umar said: It is yours. He said the like of his first saying. Umar said: Announce it for a year. He did, and when Sufyan refused, Umar placed it in the Muslims' treasury."
"18012","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il ibn Umayya informed me that Mu'awiya ibn Abd Allah ibn Badr of Juhayna said, and I heard that Abd Allah had companionship with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him that his father Abd Allah came from Syria and found a purse in it a hundred in gold, in worn-out caravan goods over which winds had passed. So he took it and brought it to Umar. Umar said to him: Announce it now at the mosque door for three days, then announce it for a year; if it is recognized, otherwise it is yours. He said: So I did, and it was not recognized, so I divided it between me and my two wives."
"18013","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayya, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: If you find a lost object, announce it at the mosque door for three days; if one comes who recognizes it, otherwise it is your affair."
"18014","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il also narrated to me that Mu'adh, or Mu'awiya ibn Abd Allah, informed him, from Abu Su'ad, and Abu Su'ad was a man of the companions, that he came from Egypt and found gold as if scattered from a caravan heading for Egypt. So he kept following the gold back toward Egypt, picking it up, until he was cut off from his companions and feared perishing, having gathered seventy dinars. So he brought them to Umar ibn al-Khattab. Umar said: Announce it for a year, otherwise it is yours. It was not recognized, so he took it."
"18015","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il also informed me that Zayd ibn al-Akhnas al-Khuza'i informed him that he said to Ibn al-Musayyib: I found a lost object; shall I give it in charity? He said: Neither you nor its owner will be rewarded. He said: Shall I hand it to the rulers? He said: Then they will devour it greedily. He said: What do you order me? He said: Announce it for a year; if it is recognized, otherwise it is yours like your wealth."
"18016","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: A man found silver coins and brought them to Ibn Umar. He said to him: Announce it. He said: I have announced it and found none who recognizes it. Shall I hand it to the emir? He said: Then he will accept it. He said: Shall I give it in charity? He said: And if its owner comes, you will guarantee it. He said: What shall I do? He said: You already saw its place, that you should not have taken it."
"18017","From al-Thawri, from Qabus ibn Abi Zabyan, from Ibn Abbas, he used to say: Do not lift the lost object; you have nothing to do with it. And he said: Leaving it is better than taking it."
"18018","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Tamim ibn Salama, or Ibrahim, he said: Shurayh passed by a dirham and did not expose himself to it."
"18019","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about the lost object: announce it; if its owner comes, otherwise give it in charity; if its owner comes, give him the choice between it and the reward."
"18020","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said to me; Ikrima, freedman of Ibn Abbas, said to me: Announce it; if it is not recognized, give it in charity; if its seeker comes, if he wills you guarantee it, and if he wills the reward is his. He said: and I heard Ata say the like of this saying of Ikrima before he heard the saying of Amr ibn Shuayb, then he moved to the saying of Amr ibn Shuayb when he heard it from him."
"18021","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Safar, that a man came to Ali and said: I found a lost object with a hundred dirhams or near it, and I announced it weakly, loving that it not be recognized. So I equipped myself with it to Siffin, and today I am well-off through it; what do you think? He said: Announce it; if its owner recognizes it, hand it to him, otherwise give it in charity; if its owner comes and loves the reward to be his, that is its way, otherwise you guarantee it and its reward is yours."
"18022","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Safar, from a man of Bani Ru'as, he said: I picked up three hundred dirhams and announced them, loving that they not be recognized. None recognized them, so I spent them. Then I came to Ali and asked him. He said: Give them in charity; if its owner comes, give him the choice; if he chooses the reward, it is his, and if he chooses the wealth, his wealth is his."
"18023","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said about the lost object: Announce it for a year; if its owner comes, otherwise give it in charity; if its owner comes after it is given in charity, give him the choice; if he chooses the reward, it is his, and if he chooses the wealth, his wealth is his."
"18024","From al-Thawri and Isra'il, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Wa'il Shaqiq ibn Salama, he said: Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud bought a slave-girl from a man for six hundred or seven hundred, then sought him for a year without finding him. Then he went out with her to al-Sudda and gave her in charity dirham by dirham on behalf of her owner; if its owner comes, give him the choice; if he chooses the reward, the reward is his, and if he chooses his wealth, his wealth is his. Then Ibn Mas'ud said: Do thus with the lost object."
"18025","From al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, about the lost object: give it in charity; if its owner comes, give him the choice; if he chooses the reward, the reward is his, and if he chooses his wealth, his wealth is his. Al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, that Shurayh did that with the lost object."
"18026","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from his wife, she said: A woman came to A'isha and said: I found a sheep. She said: Feed it, milk it, and announce it. Then she returned to her three times, and she said: Do you want me to order you to slaughter it?"
"18027","From al-Thawri, he said: Whatever spoiling is feared for, sell it and give it in charity."
"18028","From Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: Ali had a private access to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that none had, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a private access to Ali that none but he had. The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) access to Ali was that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter upon them every day; if they had anything, they offered it to him. He said: One day he entered and found nothing with them. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, Fatima said: Shame! We had accustomed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to hospitality, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out having obtained nothing. Ali said: Be quiet, woman; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knows better what is in your house than you. She said: Go; perhaps you will find something for us, or find one who lends you something. So he went out and found nothing. While walking in the market, he found a dinar and took it. Then he said: Who recognizes the dinar? He found none who recognized it. He said: By Allah, if I took this dinar and bought food with it, it would be a loan upon me; if its owner comes, I will guarantee it. A man met him and sold him food. When he had taken full food for it, he returned the dinar to him. Ali said: You have given us your food and given us a dinar. The man kept pressing him until he returned the dinar to him. When Fatima heard that from Ali, she said: Were you not ashamed to take the man's food and the dinar? He said: So I returned it, but he refused. When that food ran out, he went out with that dinar to the market, and that man met him and he bought food from him, then returned the dinar to him. Ali said to him: Man, you did this once; take your dinar. The man kept pressing Ali until he returned the dinar to him. When Ali mentioned that to Fatima, she said: Man, be ashamed; do not return to this. When that food ran out, Ali went out with that dinar, and that man met him, and he bought food from him. The man gave him the dinar, and Ali threw it away, saying: By Allah, I will not take it. So the man took it. They mentioned their affair to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: That was provision driven to you; had you not returned it, it would have sufficed you."
"18029","From Abu Bakr, from Sharik ibn Abd Allah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that Ali brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a dinar he found in the market. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Announce it three times. He did, and found none who recognized it. He returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Eat it, or it is your affair. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exchanged it for twelve dirhams; he bought with three barley, with three dates, with a dirham oil, and three remained with him. When he had eaten some of what he had, its owner came. Ali said to him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to eat it. So he went with him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning that to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: Pay it. He said: We have nothing to eat. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When something comes to us, we will pay him. He made the term of the dinar and its likes three, meaning three days, because of this hadith. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il ibn Umayya informed me that the master of the dinar was a Jew."
"18030","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, I think from Ubayd ibn Umayr, that a man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab having found a bag with sawiq. He ordered him to announce it three times, then he came to him and said: None recognized it. Umar said: Take, boy! This is better than beasts taking it and winds scattering it."
"18031","From Ma'mar, from Abd Allah ibn Muslim, brother of al-Zuhri, he said: I saw Ibn Umar find a date in the road; he took it and ate half of it, then a poor man met him and he gave him the other half."
"18032","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Talha ibn Musarrif, that Umar passed by a date on the road and ate it."
"18033","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Talha, from Anas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a date on the road and said: Were I not afraid it is of charity, I would have eaten it."
"18034","From Ibn Uyayna, from Malik ibn Mighwal, he said: I heard a woman say: Ali picked up pomegranate grains, or a grain, from the ground and ate it."
"18035","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If it is a small thing, announce it for days; I heard him name five dirhams."
"18036","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata asked about the whip, the waterskin, the sandals, and the like, which the traveler finds, and he says: Enjoy it."
"18037","Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Tawus, that his father saw no harm in sandals, a waterskin, and a whip; one enjoys them if found."
"18038","From Ma'mar, from Dimam, from Jabir ibn Zayd: he saw no harm in the whip and the trifle, as if saying: the thing the traveler finds may enjoy it."
"18039","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: No harm for the traveler to enjoy the whip, sticks, and trifles if he finds them."
"18040","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was dividing a share, Ibn Dhi al-Khuwaysira al-Tamimi came to him and said: Be just, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Woe to you! Who will be just if I am not just? Umar ibn al-Khattab said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to strike his neck. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave him! He has companions before whose prayer one of you despises his prayer, and his fasting before their fasting. They pass through religion as the arrow passes through the game; he looks at its fletchings and finds nothing in them, then looks at its shaft and finds nothing in it, then looks at its sinew binding and finds nothing in it, having outrun dung and blood. Their sign is a black man with one hand, or he said: his breasts, like a woman's breast, or like flesh wobbling. They emerge at a lull among people. Then revealed about them: And of them are those who criticize you in charities, to the verse. Abu Sa'id said: I testify I heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I testify that when Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, killed them while I was with him, the man was brought upon the description the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described."
"18041","From Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, he said: Salama ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb al-Juhani informed me that he was in the army with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that marched to the Khawarij. He said: O people! I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will emerge people of my umma who recite the Qur'an; your recitation is nothing beside theirs, nor your prayer beside theirs, nor your fasting beside theirs. They recite the Qur'an thinking it is for them, while it is against them. Their prayer does not pass their throats. They pass through Islam as the arrow passes through the game. If the army that strikes them knew what was decreed for them on their Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tongue, they would rely and leave work. The sign is a man with an upper arm and no forearm, on his arm like a nipple with white hairs. So do you go to Mu'awiya and Sham folk leaving these to succeed you in your homes and wealth? By Allah, I hope these are the people, for they shed sacred blood and raided people's cattle. March in Allah's name. Salama ibn Kuhayl said: Zayd ibn Wahb made me alight at a lodging, until he said: We passed a bridge. He said: When we met, over the Khawarij that day was Abd Allah ibn Wahb al-Rasibi. He said to them: Cast the spears and draw swords from scabbards, for I fear they will adjure you as they adjured you on Harura day and you will return. So they bristled with spears and drew swords. The people fenced them with spears. Some of them were killed upon others, and of the people that day only two men were hit. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Seek the defective one among them. They did not find him. Ali rose himself until he came to people killed upon one another. He said: Take them out. They found him toward the ground. He said takbir, then said: Allah spoke truth and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conveyed. Ubayda al-Salmani rose to him and said: O Commander of the Believers! By Allah besides Whom none but He, did you hear this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by Allah besides Whom none but He, until he adjured him three times while he swore. From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, like the hadith of al-Zuhri from Abu Salama. Jabir said: and I testify I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I testify that when Ali killed them while I was with him, the man was brought upon the description the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described."
"18042","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, he said: I heard Ali say when he killed the Nahrawan folk: Their sign is a man with a defective hand, or small hand, or stunted hand. So seek him. When they found him, he said: By Allah, were you not to exult, I would tell you what Allah, Blessed and Exalted, decreed on His Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tongue of merit for whoever kills them. He said: I said: Did you yourself hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Ka'ba, yes, by the Lord of the Ka'ba. He said it three times. I heard Hisham narrate the like of it, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, from Ali ibn Abi Talib."
"18043","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: When the Haruriyya arbitrated, Ali asked what they say. He said: They say: No judgment but Allah's. He said: Judgment belongs to Allah, and on earth are judges, but they say: No leadership. Yet people must have leadership in which the believer works, the wicked and the disbeliever enjoy, and Allah reaches the term in it."
"18044","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: When Ali heard the Muhakkima, he said: Who are these? It was said: The reciters. He said: Rather they are the ruined fault-finders. It was said: They say: No judgment but Allah's. He said: A word of truth aimed at falsehood. When he killed them, a man said: Praise Allah Who wiped them out and relieved us of them. Ali said: No, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, of them are those in men's loins whom women have not yet borne, and their last will be thieving brigands."
"18045","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan, he said: When Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, killed the Haruriyya, they said: Who are these, O Commander of the Believers, disbelievers? He said: From disbelief they fled. It was said: Hypocrites? He said: Hypocrites remember Allah but little, while these remember Allah much. It was said: What are they? He said: People struck by trial, blinded and deafened in it."
"18046","From Ma'mar, from Abu Harun, he said: My father informed me that he was with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day he killed the Haruriyya. He said: When they were killed, they were ordered to seek the man. They sought him repeatedly until they found him in a place, he said: a ruin or something, I know not what. Ali raised his hands supplicating while people supplicated. Then he put down his hands, then raised them also, then said: By Allah, Splitter of the seed and Creator of souls, were you not to exult, I would tell you what merit preceded for whoever kills them on the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tongue."
"18047","From Ma'mar, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Nadra, he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrate that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Hour will not come until two great parties fight, their claim one, and a renegade party passes between them; the nearer of the two to the truth will kill it."
"18048","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard Abu Harun narrate from Abu Sa'id the like of this, except he said: The closest of the two to Allah will kill it."
"18049","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: I heard him say: No people ever innovated an innovation but deemed the sword lawful by it."
"18050","From Ma'mar, he said: Al-Hasan said to a Khariji man: What is Islam? He said: Testimony that none but Allah is God and Muhammad is Allah's Messenger, pilgrimage to the House, fasting Ramadan, and bathing from janaba. And he mentioned things. Al-Hasan said: You kill one whose religion this is."
"18051","From Ma'mar, from Aban, he said: A Khariji band went out from Basra and were killed. So I came to Anas, and he said: Why are people alarmed? I said: A band went out. He said: What do they say? I said: They say: emigrants. He said: To Satan they emigrated. Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No hijra after the conquest?"
"18052","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ghalib, he said: When the heads of the Azariqa were brought and set on Damascus steps, Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, came. When he saw them, his eyes teared, then he said: Dogs of the Fire, dogs of the Fire. These are the worst slain killed under heaven's face, and the best slain under heaven's face are those these killed. I said: Why did your eyes tear? He said: Mercy for them; they were of Islam's folk. I said: Did you say dogs of the Fire by your opinion, or something heard? He said: I would be bold then; rather I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more than once, not twice nor thrice, and he counted times. Then he recited: On the Day faces whiten and faces blacken, until: they abide therein, Ali Imran 106-107, and recited: He it is Who sent down upon you the Book, of it firm verses, until: those of understanding. Then he took my hand and said: They are many in your land, so may Allah, the Exalted, protect you from them."
"18053","From Ja'far, from Abu Imran, from Abd Allah ibn Rabah al-Ansari, he said: It reached me that the Fire has ten doors, one of them for the Khawarij."
"18054","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid informed me, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas, with the Khawarij mentioned before him, say: They are not harder in striving than Jews and Christians though they pray. From Ibn Uyayna, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"18055","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliya al-Ziyadi, he said: I heard him say: Ali has two favors upon me; I know not which greater: Allah guided me to Islam and did not make me Haruri."
"18056","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri or another, that the Haruriyya disputed Ubayd ibn Umayr. He said: Your example with the ruler and people is but like three brothers who inherited their father; the eldest seized the two brothers' inheritance, so the middle said to the youngest: Rise, let us take our wealth from him. He refused and said: I leave him to Allah. So the middle attacked the youngest and killed him. Which was harsher to him, the one who killed him or the one who took his wealth? When they pressed him, he said: By Allah, were Islam not settled firm upon the ground and straight upon its pillar, you would be the folk I most feared would perish."
"18057","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be in my umma disagreement and split. There will come people who impress you, or themselves, calling to Allah while being nothing of Allah, thinking they are upon something while upon nothing. When they go out against you, kill them; their killer is nearer to Allah than they. They said: What is their mark? He said: Shaving and demeanor. He said: meaning they shave heads, and demeanor means they have humble appearance."
"18058","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, he said: I heard Ali preach, saying: O Allah, I am tired of them and they of me; I am weary of them and they of me. Relieve me of them and them of me. What prevents your most wretched from dyeing this with blood? And he placed his hand on his beard."
"18059","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, he said: When Ali saw Ibn Muljam al-Muradi, he said: I want his life, and he wants my killing; excuse yourself from your friend of Murad."
"18060","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Qutham, freedman of al-Fadl ibn Abbas, he said: He passed by al-Muradi, and Ali's daughter said: You will surely be killed. He said: You lied, by Allah; I will not be killed unless I die. Another than Abd al-Karim told me: she is Umm Kulthum bint Ali. Abd al-Karim said: Qutham, freedman of al-Fadl, informed me that Ali called Husayn and Muhammad and said: By my right, detain the man; if I die of it, bring him forward and kill him, and do not mutilate him. They cut him and burned him. Al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade them."
"18061","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abd Allah ibn Rabah al-Ansari, he said: I heard Ka'b say: The martyr has a light, and whoever fights the Haruriyya has ten lights. And he used to say: Hell has seven doors, three of them for the Haruriyya. He said: They went out in the time of Dawud the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"18062","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard Yazid al-Raqashi say: While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat with his companions, a man appeared before them and they praised him well. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In his face is a devil's touch. He came and greeted. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you tell yourself just now that none in the people is better than you? He said: Yes. Then he turned away. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is there a man among you to strike his neck? Abu Bakr said: I. He rose and returned, saying: I reached him and found he had drawn a line and was praying in it, and I could not bring myself to kill him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of you for him? Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I. He rose to him, then returned and said: O Messenger of Allah, I found him prostrating, and I could not bring myself to kill him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of you for him? Ali said: I, O Messenger of Allah! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are for him if you reach him, though I do not think you will reach him. He rose, then returned and said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, had I found him I would have brought his head. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the first horn of Satan risen in my umma, or the first horn risen of my umma. Had you killed him, no two men of you would disagree. Banu Isra'il split into seventy-one or seventy-two sects, and you will split like them or more; none of them right but one. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is this one? He said: The community; and the last are in the Fire."
"18063","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked Abd Allah ibn Salam: Into how many did Banu Isra'il split? He said: Into seventy-one or seventy-two sects. He said: My umma also will split like them, or one more; all in the Fire but one."
"18064","From al-Thawri, from his father, from Ibn Abi Nu'm, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: Ali, while in Yemen, sent the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gold dust in its soil. He divided it among Zayd al-Khayr al-Ta'i of Bani Nabhan, al-Aqra ibn Habis al-Hanzali of Bani Mujashi', Uyayna ibn Badr al-Fazari, and Alqama ibn Ulatha al-Amiri of Bani Kilab. Quraysh and Ansar were angered and said: He gives chiefs of Najd and leaves us. He said: I only conciliate them. Then a man came with deep eyes, prominent forehead, thick beard, high cheeks, shaven head. He said: O Muhammad, fear Allah. He said: Who obeys Allah if I disobey Him? He entrusts me over earth's folk, and you do not trust me? A man of the people asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to kill him; I think Khalid ibn al-Walid. He prevented him. When he turned away, he said: Of this one's stock are people who recite the Qur'an not passing their throats; they pass through Islam as the arrow passes through the game; they kill Islam's folk and leave idol folk. If I reach them, I will surely kill them as Ad were killed."
"18065","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Khaythama, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, from Ali, he said: When I narrate to you between me and you, war is deceit; when I narrate to you something from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by Allah, to fall from heaven is dearer to me than to lie. I heard him say: There will emerge peoples at the end of time, young in teeth, foolish in minds, saying the best word of creation; their faith will not pass their throats; they pass through religion as the arrow passes through the game. Wherever you meet them, kill them; in killing them is reward for their killer on Resurrection Day."
"18066","From Ikrima ibn Ammar, he said: Abu Zumayl al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: When the Haruriyya seceded in a house apart, I said to Ali: O Commander of the Believers, delay prayer; perhaps I may come to these people and speak to them. He said: I fear them for you. I said: No, if Allah, the Exalted, wills. He said: So I wore the best Yemeni clothes I could, then entered upon them while they rested at midday. I entered upon people never seeing harder striving folk; their hands like camel knees, faces marked from prostration. I entered, and they said: Welcome, Ibn Abbas! What brought you? I said: I came to narrate to you from the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); upon them revelation descended, and they know its interpretation. Some said: Do not speak to him; some said: By Allah, we will surely speak to him. I said: Tell me what you grudge against the cousin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his son-in-law, the first to believe in him, with the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with him? They said: We grudge three things. I said: What are they? They said: First, he arbitrated men in Allah's religion, though Allah said: Judgment is but Allah's. I said: What else? They said: He fought but did not enslave nor take booty; if they were disbelievers, their wealth was lawful to him, and if they were believers, their blood was forbidden to him? I said: What else? They said: He erased himself from Commander of the Believers; if he is not Commander of the Believers, he is commander of disbelievers. I said: See, if I read to you firm Book and narrate to you from your Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sunna what you deny not, will you return? They said: Yes. I said: As to your saying he arbitrated men in Allah's religion: Allah, the Exalted, says: O believers, kill not game while pilgrims, to His saying: judged by two just of you. And He said about the woman and her husband: If you fear split between them, send an arbiter of his folk and of hers. I adjure you by Allah: is arbitrating men in sparing their blood and selves and reconciling them worthier, or in a rabbit worth a quarter dirham? They said: O Allah, rather in sparing bloods and reconciling. He said: Am I out of this? They said: O Allah, yes. As to your saying he fought but did not enslave nor take booty: do you enslave your mother A'isha? Or deem lawful of her what you deem of others? Then you have disbelieved. If you claim she is not mother of believers, you have disbelieved and left Islam. Allah says: The Prophet is nearer to believers than themselves, and his wives their mothers. You waver between two misguidances; choose whichever. Am I out of this? They said: O Allah, yes. As to your saying he erased himself from Commander of the Believers: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Quraysh on Hudaybiya day to write a book between him and them. He said: Write: this is what Muhammad the Messenger of Allah decreed. They said: By Allah, if we knew you are Allah's Messenger, we would not bar you the House nor fight you; but write: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah. He said: By Allah, I am Allah's Messenger truly though you deny me. Write, Ali: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was better than Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Am I out of this? They said: O Allah, yes. Twenty thousand of them returned, and four thousand remained and were killed."
"18067","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should point at his brother with a weapon, for he knows not; perhaps Satan snatches it in his hand and puts him in a pit of Fire."
"18068","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bears arms against us is not of us. From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"18069","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bears arms against us is not of us, nor a watcher on the road."
"18070","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn al-Zubayr, he said: I heard him say: Whoever points with a weapon then puts it down, meaning he strikes with it, his blood is wasted."
"18071","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr say: Whoever raises a weapon then puts it down is wasted. He used to hold that also. And people said: If a man strikes a man with a sword but does not kill him, then says: for hatred between me and him, is his blood wasted? Ibn Tawus said: No. We said: When was this of your father's saying? He said: It was mentioned to us that people said to passersby: Give us your goods, else we strike you with the sword; that is when he said that."
"18072","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If a man raids people by night and robs them, with Ata present, and they kill him, they guarantee his blood-money, unless he has a weapon; if he has a weapon, no blood-money is due."
"18073","From Ma'mar, he said: I said to al-Zuhri: Hisham ibn Urwa informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, when governor of Medina in al-Walid's time, cut the hand of a man who struck another with a sword. Al-Zuhri laughed and said: Is this acted upon? Al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik wrote to Umar to cut the hand of a man who struck another with a sword. Al-Zuhri said: Umar called me and consulted me on cutting him. I said to him: I see you tell the truth and write to him that Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal struck Hassan ibn Thabit with a sword in the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) time, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not cut his hand; and so-and-so struck so-and-so in Marwan's time, and Marwan did not cut his hand. So Umar wrote that to him and waited a while with no reply, then al-Walid wrote to him: Hassan satirized Safwan, mentioning his mother, and something else al-Zuhri said; and you mentioned Marwan did not cut his hand, but Abd al-Malik cut his hand, so cut his hand. Al-Zuhri said: So he cut his hand for that, and it was of his sins he sought Allah's forgiveness for."
"18074","From al-Thawri, from Isa ibn al-Mughira, from Budayl ibn Wahb, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Tarif ibn Rabi'a, judge in Sham, that Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal struck Hassan with a sword, and the Ansar came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Wait tonight; if your companion heals, you retaliate, and if he dies, we hand him over to you."
"18075","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Ubayd Allah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, from Abd Allah ibn Adi al-Ansari, who narrated to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while sitting among people, was approached by a man seeking his permission, or consulting him whispering, about killing a hypocrite man, seeking his permission. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his voice, saying: Does he not testify none but Allah is God? He said: Yes, but he has no testimony. He said: Does he not testify I am Allah's Messenger? He said: Yes, but he has no testimony. He said: Does he not pray? He said: Yes, but he has no prayer. He said: Those are they I am forbidden to kill."
"18076","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, he said: Simak ibn Harb informed me, from al-Nu'man ibn Salim, from a man, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us while we were in a dome in Medina's mosque. He took the dome's pillar and began narrating to us, when a man came and whispered to him; I know not what he whispered. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go with him and kill him. When the man turned away, he called him and said: Perhaps he says none but Allah is God. He said: Yes. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go and tell them to release him, for it was revealed to me to fight people until they say none but Allah is God; when they say none but Allah is God, their blood and wealth are forbidden to me except by right, and their reckoning is upon Allah."
"18077","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me about a man who disbelieves after faith: he is called to Islam; if he refuses, he is killed. I said: How many times called? He said: I know not. I said: From whom? He said: I know not, but we heard that."
"18078","From Uthman, from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Abu al-Ala, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, that Ali sought a man who disbelieved after Islam to repent for a month; he refused, so he killed him."
"18079","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me that it reached him from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man disbelieved after faith, so he called him to Islam three times; he refused, so he killed him."
"18080","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hayyan informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: If the Muslim associates partners, he is called to Islam three times; if he refuses, his neck is struck."
"18081","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say about the man who disbelieves after faith: he is killed."
"18082","From Ma'mar, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari informed me, from his father, he said: Majza'a ibn Thawr, or Shaqiq ibn Thawr, came to Umar announcing Tustar's conquest. He did not find him in Medina; he was away on his land. So he came to him. When he neared the wall he was in, he said takbir, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, heard his takbir and said takbir. Each kept saying takbir until they met. Umar said: What have you? He said: I adjure you by Allah, O Commander of the Believers! Allah opened Tustar to us, and it is such and such, of Basra's land, fearing he would transfer it to Kufa. He said: Yes, it is of Basra's land. Hie! Was there a strange matter you tell us? He said: No, except an Arab man apostatized, so we struck his neck. Umar said: Woe to you! Why did you not wall a door upon him, open a skylight, feed him a loaf daily and water him a jug of water for three days, then offer Islam to him on the third day? Perhaps he would return. Then he said: O Allah, I was not present, nor ordered, nor knew."
"18083","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Abu Musa sent me with Tustar's conquest to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. Umar asked me; six men of Bani Bakr ibn Wa'il had apostatized from Islam and joined the polytheists. He said: What did the men of Bakr ibn Wa'il do? So I took to another talk to distract him from them. He said: What did the men of Bakr ibn Wa'il do? I said: O Commander of the Believers! People apostatized from Islam and joined the polytheists; their way is but killing. Umar said: For me to have taken them peacefully is dearer to me than the sun rising over yellow or white gold. I said: O Commander of the Believers! What would you have done with them had you taken them? He said: I would have offered them the door they exited to enter through it; if they did, I would accept from them, otherwise I would lodge them in prison."
"18084","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Qays, from Ibrahim, he said about the apostate: he is sought to repent ever. Sufyan said: this is what we take."
"18085","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: It was said: Avert hadd punishments from Muslims as able; if you find for the Muslim an outlet, avert it from him. For a Muslim judge to err in pardon is better than to err in punishment."
"18086","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought Nabhan's repentance four times."
"18087","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Nothing less than his blood is accepted from one who returns from his religion."
"18088","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Abd Allah ibn Urwa informed me that Uthman, while besieged, ascended in his privy and heard them mention killing him, wanting none else. So he descended and said: I heard them wanting a matter my tongue feared not to be humbled by, nor breasts opened by. The Muslim's blood is lawful only for three: disbelief after faith, adultery after ihsan, or killing a soul without a soul."
"18089","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id, he said: Uthman ibn Affan said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Muslim's blood is not lawful but for three: to fornicate after ihsan, then stoned; or to kill a man, then killed; or to disbelieve after Islam, then killed."
"18090","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: When Uthman was besieged, he said: The Muslim's blood is not lawful but for one of three: to kill, then killed; or to fornicate after ihsan; or to disbelieve after Islam."
"18091","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murra, from Masruq, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us as my station among you, saying: By Him besides Whom none but He, the blood of a man testifying none but Allah is God and I am Allah's Messenger is not lawful but for one of three: soul for soul, the married adulterer, and the leaver of Islam parting the community."
"18092","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abu Burda, he said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal came to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari in Yemen, and lo a man with him. He said: What is this? He said: A man was Jewish, accepted Islam, then Judaized, and we have wanted him to Islam for, I think he said, two months. Mu'adh said: By Allah, I will not sit until you strike his neck. So his neck was struck. Then Mu'adh said: Allah and His Messenger decreed that whoever returns from his religion, kill him, or he said: whoever changes his religion, kill him. Ma'mar said: and I heard Qatada say: Mu'adh said: By Allah, I will not sit until you strike his neck."
"18093","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever changes from his religion, or he said: returns, kill him, and do not punish with Allah's punishment, meaning the Fire."
"18094","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, from his father, he said: Ibn Mas'ud seized people who apostatized from Islam of Iraq's folk and wrote to Umar about them. He wrote to him: Offer them the true religion and testimony none but Allah is God; if they accept, free them, and if not, kill them. Some accepted and he left them; some did not accept and he killed them."
"18095","From Ibn Uyayna, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: A man came to Ibn Mas'ud and said: I passed a mosque of Bani Hanifa's mosques and heard them recite what Allah did not send: the grinders grinding, the kneaders kneading, the bakers baking, the eaters gobbling. He said: So Ibn Mas'ud brought forward Ibn al-Nawwaha their imam and killed him, and deemed the rest many. He said: I will not let Satan flow through them today; march them to Sham until Allah grants them repentance or plague kills them."
"18096","He said: and Isma'il informed me, from Qays, that Ibn Mas'ud said: This Ibn al-Nawwaha came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Musaylima having sent him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were I a killer of envoys, I would have killed him."
"18097","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, he said: Ali was brought an old man who was Christian, accepted Islam, then apostatized from Islam. Ali said to him: Perhaps you apostatized only to gain inheritance, then return to Islam? He said: No. He said: Perhaps you betrothed a woman and they refused to marry you, so you wanted to marry her then return to Islam? He said: No. He said: Return to Islam! He said: No, not until I meet Christ. So he ordered him and his neck was struck, and his inheritance was handed to his Muslim children."
"18098","From Ibn Uyayna, from Sulayman al-Shami, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, that al-Mustawrid al-Ijli Christianized after Islam. Utba ibn Farqad sent him to Ali; he sought him to repent, but he did not repent, so he killed him. The Christians sought his corpse for thirty thousand, but Ali refused and burned him. Ibn Uyayna said: And Ammar al-Duhni informed me that Ali sought him to repent while wanting prayer, saying: I seek Allah's help against you. He said: And I seek Christ's help against you. Ali inclined to his neck and lo a cross, so he cut it and said: Kill him, servants of Allah. When Ali entered prayer, he brought a man forward and left, then told people he did that not for a ritual impurity, but he had touched these filths and loved to renew wudu."
"18099","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ibn Ubayd ibn al-Abras, that Ali sought al-Mustawrid al-Ijli to repent; he had apostatized from Islam but refused, so he struck him with his foot and the people killed him."
"18100","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn Mukhariq, that Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr wrote to Ali asking him about two Muslims who became heretics. He wrote to him: If they repent, otherwise strike their necks."
"18101","From Ma'mar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, that Urwa wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about a man who accepted Islam then apostatized. Umar wrote to him: Ask him about Islam's laws; if he knew them, offer him Islam; if he refuses, strike his neck. If he did not know them,make the jizya heavy and leave him."
"18102","From Ma'mar, he said: People of Jazira informed me that people accepted Islam, then stayed but little till they apostatized. Maymun ibn Mihran wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about them, and Umar wrote to him: Return the jizya upon them and leave them."
"18103","Ibn Uyayna informed us, from Ammar al-Duhni, he said: I heard Abu al-Tufayl say: Ali sent Ma'qil al-Sulami to Bani Najiya. He found them three kinds: a kind who were Christians then accepted Islam, a kind who remained on Christianity, and a kind who accepted Islam then returned from Islam to Christianity. He set a sign with his companions: when you see it, put weapons into the kind who accepted Islam then returned from Islam. He showed them the sign, and they put weapons into them; they killed their fighters and captive their offspring. He sold them to Masqala for a hundred thousand; he paid fifty, and fifty remained. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, allowed that. Masqala joined Mu'awiya, may Allah be pleased with him, and freed them; Ali allowed their freeing. He came to Masqala's house and disheveled it. They came to him after that, and he said: Your companion has joined your enemy; bring him to me, I will take your right for you."
"18104","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When Abu Bakr sent to fight the ridda folk, he said: Ascertain; whichever settlement you hear the adhan in, refrain, for the adhan is faith's emblem."
"18105","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: The ridda folk used to come to Abu Bakr saying: Give us weapons to fight with. He would give them weapons, and they would fight him. Abbas ibn Mirdas said: Do you take his weapons and fight him? In that for you are sins from Allah, he says: exemplary punishment."
"18106","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, he said: When the ridda folk apostatized in Abu Bakr's time, Umar said: How do you fight people, Abu Bakr! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am ordered to fight people until they say none but Allah is God; when they say none but Allah is God, they protect from me their blood and wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah? Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I will surely fight whoever separates prayer and zakat, for zakat is wealth's right. By Allah, if they withheld a young goat they paid the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would fight them for it. Umar said: By Allah, it was only that I saw Allah expanded Abu Bakr's breast for fighting, so I knew it was the truth."
"18107","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Ubayd Allah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, from al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah! If I and a polytheist man exchange two blows and he cuts my hand, then when I aim at him to strike him he says: None but Allah is God, shall I kill him or leave him? He said: Rather leave him. I said: Even if he cut my hand? He said: Even so. I reviewed him twice or thrice, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you kill him after he said none but Allah is God, you are like him before he said it, and he is like you before you kill him. He was a Kinda man, ally of Bani Zuhra."
"18108","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah ibn Mawhab, from Qabisa ibn Dhu'ayb, he said: A man of the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) companions raided a routed detachment, overcame a polytheist man among them, and when he wanted to raise the sword over him, the man said: None but Allah is God. He did not refrain from him until he killed him. The man found in himself over killing him. He mentioned his hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: He only said it seeking refuge. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not pierce his heart? The tongue only expresses the heart. They stayed but little till that killer man died. He was buried, and morning found him on earth's surface. His family came and told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Bury him. He was buried also, and morning found him on earth's surface. His family told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earth refused to accept him, so cast him in a mountain cave."
"18109","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Khalid ibn al-Walid to Bani, I think he said: Jadhima. He called them to Islam, but they did not do well to say: we accepted; they kept saying: we left our religion. Khalid made killing and capture. He handed each man of us a captive, until one day Khalid ordered each of us to kill his captive. Ibn Umar said: By Allah, I will not kill my captive, nor will a man of my companions kill his captive. We came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned Khalid's deed to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands and said: O Allah, I dissociate myself to You from what Khalid did; O Allah, I dissociate myself to You from what Khalid did."
"18110","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Abu Qatada said: We went out in the Ridda until we reached tent folk, with the sun risen toward setting. We leveled spears at them. They said: Who are you? We said: We are Allah's servants. They said: And we are Allah's servants. Khalid ibn al-Walid captured them until morning, when he ordered their necks be struck. Abu Qatada said: So I said: Fear Allah, Khalid! This is not lawful for you. He said: Sit; this is nothing of you. Abu Qatada used to swear never to raid with Khalid ever. The bedouins encouraged him to kill them for booty. That was about Malik ibn Nuwayra."
"18111","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Khallad informed me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that a man asked Abd Allah ibn Umar, or Ibn Amr, I doubt: A man charged me with a sword, the sword fell from him, so I took it and killed him. He said: Then you will meet Allah having killed a soul. He said: See, had he killed me? He said: Then he would meet Allah having killed a soul."
"18112","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman was one of Bani Abs and was Ansari. He fought with his father al-Yaman on Uhud day with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hard fighting. The Muslims surrounded al-Yaman and struck him with swords, while Hudhayfa kept saying: My father! They understood not until he reached them, with the people showering him with swords, and they killed him. Hudhayfa said: Allah forgive you, and He is Most Merciful of merciful. It reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it increased him in good with him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money."
"18113","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about the woman who disbelieves after Islam, he said: She is sought to repent; if she repents, otherwise she is killed."
"18114","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, about the woman who apostatizes, he said: She is sought to repent; if she repents, otherwise she is killed. From al-Thawri, from some of his companions, from Ibrahim, the like of it. Al-Hasan said: She is enslaved and coerced."
"18115","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: She is captive and sold; likewise Abu Bakr did with the ridda women, selling them."
"18116","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote about an umm walad who Christianized: she be sold in a land of her birth upon her, and not sold to her religion's folk."
"18117","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sold her at Dumat al-Jandal to other than her religion's folk."
"18118","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Razin, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The woman who apostatizes is detained, not killed."
"18119","From al-Thawri, he said: We heard that puberty's least is fourteen and most eighteen; when hadd punishments come, we take the most. Abd al-Razzaq said: People are upon it, and we take it."
"18120","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salama, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he was brought a slave-girl who had not menstruated and stole, and he did not cut her."
"18121","From al-Thawri, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, he said: Ibn Abi al-Sa'ba courted a woman in his poetry, and it was raised to Umar ibn al-Khattab. He said: Look at his waist-wrapper; it had not grown hair. He said: Had you grown hair, I would have lashed you the hadd."
"18122","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: Uthman was brought a boy who stole. He said: Look at his waist-wrapper. They looked and found him ungrown, so he did not cut him."
"18123","From Ma'mar, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Salim ibn Abd Allah were asked: When is the boy hadd-punished? They said: When he grows hair."
"18124","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulayka say: Ibn al-Zubayr was brought a page of Umar ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Rabi'a who stole. Ibn al-Zubayr ordered him measured by spans and he was found six spans, so he cut him. And Ibn al-Zubayr told us then that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Iraq about a boy of Bani Amir called Numayla who stole while a boy. Umar wrote: Span him; if he reaches six spans, cut him. They spanned him and he lacked a fingertip, so they left him. So he was named Numayla. Later he mastered Iraq's folk."
"18125","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: No cutting on him until he has a wet dream."
"18126","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: There is no hadd and no retaliation upon one who has not reached puberty."
"18127","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, he said: I do not think my father but used to say that."
"18128","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no cutting upon one who has not had a wet dream, whether for theft or hadd, and the woman is likewise so long as she has not menstruated; and one who heard al-Hasan informed me that he said that."
"18129","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Atiyya al-Qurazi, he said: I was among those concerning whom Sa'd ibn Mu'adh gave judgment, and I was brought forward to be killed, then a man of the people pulled away my waist-wrapper and they saw that I had not grown pubic hair, so I was cast among the captives; from al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik, from Atiyya, likewise."
"18130","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, Umar ibn al-Khattab said: There is no retaliation and no requital for wounds, nor for killing, nor hadd, nor exemplary punishment upon one who has not reached puberty, until he knows what he has in Islam and what is upon him."
"18131","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Juz' ibn Mu'awiya, uncle of al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, and he was a governor of Umar: Kill every sorcerer; and Bajala corresponded with Juz', Bajala said: So we sent and found three sorceresses, and we struck their necks."
"18132","From Ma'mar and Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Bajala narrating to Abu al-Sha'tha and Amr ibn Aws at the terrace of Zamzam, during the rule of Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr, he said: I was a scribe for Juz', uncle of al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, and the letter of Umar came to us a year before his death: Kill every sorcerer, and separate every mahram among the Magians, and forbid them humming; so we killed three sorceresses; he said: And he prepared much food and displayed the sword, then he summoned the Magians, and they threw down about a mule-load or two mule-loads of silver toothpicks with which they used to eat, and they ate without humming."
"18133","He said: And Umar had not taken the jizya from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf testified that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took it from the Magians of Hajar."
"18134","From Abdullah or Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: That a slave-girl of Hafsa bewitched her, and she confessed that, so Hafsa ordered Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd concerning her and he killed her; and Uthman disapproved of that to her, so Ibn Umar said: What do you disapprove against the Mother of the Believers concerning a woman who practiced sorcery and confessed? Then Uthman fell silent."
"18135","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Bajala al-Taymi say: Umar ibn al-Khattab found a mushaf in the lap of a boy in the mosque containing: The Prophet is closer to the believers than their own selves, and he is their father; so he said: Erase it, boy; and he said: By Allah, I shall not erase it while it is in the mushaf of Ubayy ibn Ka'b; so he went off to Ubayy, and he said to him: The Qur'an kept me busy, while bargaining in the markets kept you busy, when you display your cloak upon your neck at the door of Ibn al-Ajma'."
"18136","He said: And Umar would not take the jizya from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf testified that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took it from the Magians of Hajar."
"18137","He said: And Umar wrote to Juz' ibn Mu'awiya, uncle of al-Ahnaf ibn Qays: Kill every sorcerer, and separate every woman from her kinswoman in the Book of Allah, and let none hum; and that was a year before he died; he said: So we sent and found three sorceresses, and we struck their necks, and we would ask a man: Who is with you? And he would say: My mother, my sister, my daughter, and they would be separated from him; and Juz' prepared much food and placed the sword in his lap, and said: Let none hum, or I shall strike his neck; so they threw down silver toothpicks with which they used to eat, a mule-load of what filled it."
"18138","He said: As for the affair of Abu Bustan, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Jundub: Jundub, and what is Jundub, he shall strike a blow by which he separates truth from falsehood; and Abu Bustan was performing below the fortress in the presence of al-Walid ibn Uqba, and he was the amir of Kufa, while the people thought he was upon the wall of the palace, meaning the middle of the palace; so Jundub said: Woe to you, O people! Is he not sporting with you? By Allah, he is at the bottom of the palace, he is only at the bottom of the palace; then he went off and wrapped himself with the sword, then he struck him; and among them are those who say he killed him, and among them are those who say he did not kill him; and the sorcery departed from him; so Abu Bustan said: Allah has benefited me by your blow; and al-Walid ibn Uqba imprisoned him and disparaged his nephew Athiyya, and he was the horseman of the Arabs, until he attacked the keeper of the prison and killed him, and brought him out; and that is his saying: Is Jundub imprisoned in the striking-place of the sorcerers while the early Companions of the Prophet are killed? If my opinion of Ibn Salma and his people is the truth, Jundub shall be released or fought for; so he disparaged Uthman in this poem of his, and went off to the land of the Byzantines, and he remained there fighting until he died, ten years having passed of the caliphate of Mu'awiya; and Mu'awiya used to say: There is none dearer to me than Athiyya; Uthman exiled him, so I can neither grant him security nor bring him back; Abd al-Razzaq said: And Athiyya is the one who composed the poetry and who struck Abu Bustan the sorcerer."
"18139","From Malik, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Amra bint Abd al-Rahman, that Aisha had freed a slave-girl of hers after her own death, then she bewitched her, and she confessed that; she said: I loved freedom; so Aisha ordered her nephew to sell her to the Bedouins among those who treat their possession harshly; she said: And buy with her price a slave and free him; so he did."
"18140","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu al-Rijal, from Amra, she said: Aisha fell ill and her illness grew long, so the sons of her brother went to a man, and they mentioned her illness to him, and he said: You are telling me news of a bewitched woman; he said: So they went to look, and there was a slave-girl of hers who had bewitched her, and she had already granted her freedom after her death; so she asked her, and she said: What did you intend by me? And she said: I intended that you should die so that I would be freed; she said: Then it is binding upon me for Allah that you shall be sold to the harshest of the Arabs in ownership; so she sold her, and she ordered that her price be spent upon another."
"18141","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, that Sa'd ibn Qays, or Qays ibn Sa'd, killed a sorcerer."
"18142","From Ibn Uyayna, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The hadd of the sorcerer is a stroke with the sword."
"18143","From Ibrahim, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever learns anything of sorcery, little or much, his last covenant with Allah shall be severed."
"18144","From Ibrahim, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Yazid ibn Ruman, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was brought a sorcerer, and he said: Detain him, then if his victim dies, kill him."
"18145","From Abd al-Rahman, from al-Muthanna, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that Umar ibn al-Khattab seized a sorcerer and buried him up to his chest, then he left him until he died."
"18146","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Bajala, that Umar wrote to his governor: Kill every sorcerer; then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj at the beginning of the chapter."
"18147","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Hafsa was bewitched, so she ordered Ubaydullah, her brother, and he killed two sorceresses."
"18148","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: He stole the first time? He said: His palm is cut; I said: Then what is their saying, his fingers? He said: I have known nothing but the cutting of the whole palm; I said: Then he stole the second time? He said: I do not see cutting except for the first theft, the hand ever; Allah, blessed and exalted is He, said: Cut off their hands, and had He wished He would have ordered the foot, and Allah was not forgetful."
"18149","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Ikrima, that Umar used to cut the foot from its joint, and that Ali, from other than Ikrima, used to cut the foot; Amr gestured to me to its half."
"18150","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: That Ali used to cut the hand from the fingers, and the foot from half the sole."
